{ Welcome to the French defence: you build a wall of pawns in the middle, and when he pushes past it, you knock his pawns down from the side. Against d4 first, you would need another opening. }
1. e4 { He opens with his king pawn. The French starts with a small step: your king pawn to e6, so your d pawn can jump to d5 next. [%cal Ye7e6] } 1... e6 { The pawn on e6 guards d5, so your d pawn can go there next and hit his king pawn. [%cal Ge6d5] } (1... e5 { Very good too, but that is a different opening, where your king pawn goes two squares. } 2. Nf3 { Level: a fine game, just not the French, where the king pawn stops one square short. [%csl Be5] }) (1... c5 { The Sicilian: just as good, and it has a study of its own. } 2. Nf3 d6 { Level, and a whole different fight. Today we learn the French, where the king pawn stops one square short. [%csl Bc5] }) (1... c6 { The Caro-Kann, a close cousin of the French, where the c pawn backs up the d pawn instead. } 2. d4 d5 { Level: your light bishop can still get out on this road, but that is another opening. [%csl Bc6] }) (1... d5 { Also playable: hitting his pawn at once is the Scandinavian, another opening. } 2. exd5 Nf6 3. Nf3 { About level: he is a pawn up for now, and you must spend time winning it back. [%csl Rd5] }) 2. d4 { He takes the middle with two pawns. Against a knight move, a bishop move, a quiet pawn to d3 or his f pawn two squares, the answer is the same: your d pawn two squares. [%cal Yd7d5] } 2... d5 { That is the French: pawns on e6 and d5, and his king pawn is under attack. He must push it, trade it or guard it. [%cal Gd5e4] } (2... c5 { Also playable: you hit the middle from the side, and his d pawn simply walks past. } 3. d5 Nf6 4. Nc3 { About level: his pawn on d5 takes space, and this is not the French any more. [%csl Rd5] }) (2... b6 { The bishop wants b7, but that is slow while his two pawns rule the middle. } 3. Bd3 { Slightly better for him: his pieces come out fast, and your bishop on c8 still sits at home. [%csl Rc8] }) (2... d6 { Also playable, but a quieter set-up: your d pawn stops on d6 and leaves him the middle. } 3. Nf3 Nf6 { About level: his pawns on d4 and e4 hold the middle, and yours wait behind them. [%csl Rd6] }) (2... c6 { The c pawn goes first, a different set-up. } 3. Bd3 { Slightly better for him: you spent a whole move on the c pawn, and he spent it bringing out a piece. [%csl Rc6] }) (2... Nf6 { The knight hits his king pawn, but the pawn just pushes on and kicks it. } 3. e5 Nd5 4. c4 { Slightly better for him: his pawns chase your knight around, and each push gains space. [%cal Rc4d5] }) 3. e5 { He pushes past. His pawns now stand corner to corner, the back one guarding the front: that is a pawn chain. Attack it at its back pawn, the base: hit d4 with your c pawn. [%cal Yc7c5,Rd4e5] } 3... c5 { Your c pawn hits d4, the base of his pawn chain. Take that base away, and the pawn on e5 loses its guard. [%cal Gc5d4] } (3... Bd7 { Just as good: the bishop comes out first, and the c pawn hits the base next move. } 4. Nf3 c5 { Level: the same fight for the base, with your bishop already on d7. [%csl Bd7] }) (3... Ne7 { Just as good: the king knight is on its way to f5, to hit d4 from there. } 4. c3 c5 5. Nf3 { About level: your c pawn hits d4 after all, and the knight can still reach f5. [%cal Gc5d4,Ge7f5] }) (3... Nc6 { The knight gets in front of your c pawn, so that pawn can no longer hit the base. } 4. Nf3 f6 5. Bb5 { Slightly better for him: your knight blocks your own c pawn, and his bishop pins it to your king. [%cal Rb5e8] }) (3... f6 { Hitting the front of his chain first is too early: it loosens your king side before a single piece of yours is out. } 4. Bd3 { Slightly better for him: his bishop aims at your loose king side, and the base of his chain, d4, was never touched. [%csl Rd4] }) (3... b6 { The bishop wants b7, but that diagonal runs straight into your own pawn. } 4. h4 { Slightly better for him: your bishop has nothing to hit from b7, and the base on d4 stands untouched. [%csl Rd4] }) 4. c3 { He props up d4 with his c pawn, so that c pawn is now the base. Keep hitting d4 anyway: once pawns trade there, the base is back where it was. Bring your queen knight out. [%cal Yb8c6] } 4... Nc6 { Your knight and your c pawn both hit d4 now, and he guards it twice. Against his f pawn two squares, the plan stays the same. [%cal Gc6d4,Gc5d4] } (4... Qb6 { Just as good: the queen comes first. When his knight comes out and yours joins it, you are back on the main road. } 5. Nf3 Nc6 { Level: the very same position as the main line, reached by another road. [%csl Gb6] }) (4... Bd7 { Just as good: a quieter plan with the bishop first. } 5. Nf3 { Level: the same pawns, and your queen stays at home for now. [%csl Bd7] }) (4... cxd4 { Also fine: trade at once, and his c pawn takes back. } 5. cxd4 { About level: the chain is two pawns long again, and its base on d4 has no pawn to guard it. [%csl Gd4] }) (4... Ne7 { Just as good: the king knight heads out first. } 5. Nf3 Nbc6 6. Na3 { Level: both your knights are out, and the fight for d4 goes on. [%csl Gd4] }) 5. Nf3 { His knight guards d4 a third time. Bring your queen out: she lines up behind your c pawn and hits his pawn on b2. [%cal Rf3d4,Yd8b6] } 5... Qb6 { Your queen stands behind the c pawn, ready for the trade on d4, and she hits b2. So his dark bishop cannot leave home without dropping that pawn. [%cal Gb6b2] } (5... Bd7 { Just as good: the bishop first, a quieter way to play. } 6. Be2 { Level: the same pawns, and your queen can still come to b6 later. [%csl Bd7] }) (5... Nge7 { Just as good: the king knight is on its way to f5. } 6. Bd3 cxd4 7. cxd4 { Level: your knight can reach f5 from here and hit d4 again. [%cal Ge7f5] }) (5... cxd4 { Also fine: trade now, and let his c pawn take back. } 6. cxd4 Nge7 { About level: d4 is the base of his chain again, and both your knights can reach it. [%csl Gd4] }) (5... Nh6 { The knight goes to the edge a move too early, where his bishop can take it. } 6. Bxh6 gxh6 { About level, but his bishop took your knight and broke your king side pawns. [%csl Rh6] }) 6. Be2 { His bishop comes out, ready to castle. Now trade on d4: your c pawn takes. [%cal Yc5d4] } 6... cxd4 { Your pawn takes. If his knight takes back, his pawn on e5 loses its guard; if his c pawn does, no pawn is left to guard d4. [%csl Gd4] } (6... Nge7 { Just as good: the knight heads for f5 first. } 7. dxc5 Qxc5 8. b4 { About level: he took on c5 and pushed his b pawn at your queen, but your knight still reaches f5. [%cal Ge7f5] }) (6... Nh6 { Just as good: the knight heads for f5 at once. } 7. Bxh6 gxh6 { About level: his bishop took your knight and broke your king side pawns, but you keep both bishops. [%csl Rh6] }) (6... Bd7 { A little slow: the bishop comes out before the trade. } 7. O-O cxd4 8. cxd4 { About level, but he castled first, and you are a step behind the main line. [%csl Gd4] }) 7. cxd4 { He takes back, and now only his knight and queen guard d4. Your king knight heads for f5 by way of the edge. [%cal Yg8h6,Gh6f5,Gf5d4] } 7... Nh6 { The knight is one jump from f5. If his b pawn steps to b3, check with your dark bishop first. [%cal Gh6f5] } (7... Nge7 { Just as good: the knight goes to f5 through e7 instead. } 8. Na3 { About level: the knight still reaches f5, only by the other road. [%cal Ge7f5] }) (7... Bd7 { Developing, but slow: the knight should hurry toward f5. } 8. Nc3 Nge7 9. Na4 { About level, but his knight hits your queen first, and you are a step behind. [%cal Ra4b6] }) (7... Bb4+ { The check looks active, but his knight simply blocks and comes out at the same time. } 8. Nc3 Nge7 9. O-O { Slightly better for him: the check only helped him develop, and your bishop on b4 now blocks your queen's road to b2. [%cal Rb6b2] }) 8. Nc3 { His knight comes out, and now his dark bishop could take your knight and wreck your king side pawns. Jump to f5. [%csl Rh6][%cal Yh6f5] } 8... Nf5 { Three of your pieces hit d4 now: both knights and the queen. He guards it only twice: if he castles, that pawn falls, and if his bishop comes to e3, your knight takes it. [%cal Gf5d4,Gc6d4,Gb6d4] } (8... Bd7 { Developing, but it leaves your knight on h6 for his bishop. } 9. Bxh6 gxh6 { Better for him: his bishop took your knight, and your king side pawns are broken for good. [%csl Rh6] }) (8... Be7 { The bishop guards nothing that matters, and the knight is still on h6. } 9. Bxh6 gxh6 10. O-O { Better for him: your knight stayed on h6, where his bishop took it and split your pawns. [%csl Rh6] }) (8... Bb4 { The pin on his knight looks strong, but his bishop can still take your knight on h6. } 9. Bxh6 gxh6 { Better for him: his bishop took your knight, and your king side pawns are wrecked. [%csl Rh6] }) (8... a6 { A quiet pawn move gives his bishop time. } 9. Bxh6 gxh6 10. a3 { Better for him: his bishop took on h6, and your pawns there are split in two. [%csl Rh6] }) 9. Na4 { His knight jumps at your queen. Step aside with a check: queen to a5. Against a bishop move to b5, you would block with your bishop instead. [%cal Ra4b6,Yb6a5] } 9... Qa5+ { Check along the diagonal to his king. If his knight goes back to c3, your queen goes back to b6, and he can repeat moves for a draw. [%cal Ga5e1] } (9... Qd8 { The queen goes all the way home, and your pressure on d4 goes with her. } 10. O-O Bd7 11. a3 { About level, but your queen went home, and he had time to castle. [%csl Rd8] }) (9... Qc7 { The queen steps back to c7, where she no longer hits d4. } 10. O-O { About level, but d4 is safe again, and his king is castled. [%csl Gd4] }) (9... Qb4+ { A check too, but his bishop blocks and chases your queen again. } 10. Bd2 Qe7 11. Bc3 { Slightly better for him: your queen ran twice, and his bishop now guards d4. [%cal Rc3d4] }) (9... Bb4+ { The bishop check looks strong, but his king steps aside and your queen is still attacked. } 10. Kf1 Qd8 11. h4 { Slightly better for him: your queen went home anyway, and your bishop on b4 has nothing to hit. [%csl Rb4] }) 10. Bd2 { He blocks with his bishop, and now it hits your queen. Block back with your dark bishop, which hits his bishop in turn. Against his king to f1, your b pawn would chase the knight. [%cal Rd2a5,Yf8b4] } 10... Bb4 { Your bishop blocks the attack on your queen and hits his bishop on d2. If he takes on b4, your queen takes back with check. [%cal Gb4d2] } (10... Qd8 { Just as good: the queen steps back, and your pieces still point at d4. } 11. Bc3 { Level: the queen went back, but the fight for d4 goes on. [%csl Gd4] }) (10... Qc7 { The queen runs to c7, where she does nothing for d4. } 11. Rc1 Qd8 12. Bc3 { Slightly better for him: your queen went back and forth, and his rook came to the c column. [%csl Rc1] }) (10... Nfxd4 { Grabbing d4 forgets that his bishop hits your queen. } 11. Bxa5 Nxf3+ 12. Bxf3 Nxa5 { Lost for you: his bishop took your queen, and a bishop and a pawn do not pay for her. [%csl Ra5] }) 11. Bc3 { His bishop steps up and offers a trade. His knight on a4 is guarded only by his queen: chase it with your b pawn. Had his knight run back instead, d4 would have fallen. [%cal Yb7b5] } 11... b5 { Your b pawn hits his knight, and it has no safe square to run to. If his bishop takes on b4, your queen takes back with check. [%cal Gb5a4] } (11... Bxc3+ { Just as good: trade bishops first, with check. } 12. Nxc3 Qb6 { Level: your queen is back on b6, hitting d4 and his b pawn again. [%cal Gb6d4,Gb6b2] }) (11... Bd7 { Just as good: develop first, and chase the knight later. } 12. a3 { Level: his pawn hits your bishop, but you can trade it on c3 with check. [%cal Gb4c3] }) (11... O-O { Just as good: your king gets safe first. } 12. O-O Bd7 { Level: both kings are castled, and d4 is still your target. [%csl Gd4] }) 12. a3 { He hits your bishop with his a pawn, and his knight is still attacked. Trade the bishops first, with check. [%cal Yb4c3] } 12... Bxc3+ { You trade bishops with check. Taking back with the pawn would lose his knight on a4 to your b pawn. [%csl Ra4] } (12... Qxa4 { Taking the knight lets his pawn take your bishop, and then the queens come off. } 13. axb4 Qxd1+ 14. Kxd1 { Slightly better for him: the material is even, but he kept both bishops against your one. [%csl Rb4] }) (12... bxa4 { The pawn takes the knight, but then his pawn takes your bishop with an attack on your queen. } 13. axb4 Qd8 14. Qxa4 { Much better for him: he won back the knight's worth and more, and your pawns are scattered. [%csl Ra4] }) (12... Be7 { The bishop steps back, and that opens the line from his bishop to your queen. } 13. Bxa5 { Lost for you: his bishop from c3 took your queen. [%csl Ra5] }) 13. Nxc3 { His knight takes back on c3. Push your b pawn again: it hits the knight and the pawn on a3 at the same time. [%cal Yb5b4] } 13... b4 { Your pawn hits the knight on c3 and the pawn on a3. His best answer is to take it. [%cal Gb4c3,Gb4a3] } (13... O-O { Playable too: castle, and let him take the b pawn for now. } 14. Bxb5 Rb8 { Level: a pawn down for now, but your rook on b8 hits his bishop on b5. [%cal Gb8b5] }) (13... a6 { Playable, if slower: guard your b pawn first. } 14. Bd3 { Level: your pawn on b5 is safe, and both knights still aim at d4. [%csl Gd4] }) (13... Bd7 { A little slow: his king side pawns get moving. } 14. g4 Nfe7 15. b4 { Slightly better for him: his pawn chased your knight away from f5, and d4 is safe now. [%csl Rg4] }) 14. axb4 { He takes your pawn, and his rook on a1 now looks straight at your queen. Take back with the queen. [%cal Ra1a5,Ya5b4] } 14... Qxb4 { Your queen takes back and hits two pawns at once, b2 and d4. Against a queen move that offers a trade, take the trade. [%cal Gb4b2,Gb4d4] } (14... Qb6 { Stepping back leaves him a pawn up. } 15. Bb5 O-O { Slightly better for him: he keeps the extra pawn on b4, and his bishop hits your knight on c6. [%csl Rb4] }) (14... Qd8 { Going all the way back leaves him a pawn up and pushes you back. } 15. b5 Nce7 16. h4 { Much better for him: a pawn up, and his b pawn drove your knight away. [%csl Rb5] }) 15. Bb5 { His bishop hits your knight on c6, and nothing guards it. Cover it with your light bishop. Had he castled instead, d4 would fall. [%cal Rb5c6,Yc8d7] } 15... Bd7 { The bishop guards your knight, and the light bishop that was shut in behind your pawns is out at last. [%cal Gd7c6] } (15... Bb7 { The bishop guards the knight from b7, but there it only stares at your own pawn on d5. } 16. g4 Nh6 17. Qd2 { Slightly better for him: his pawn chased your knight to the edge, and your bishop on b7 has nothing to do. [%csl Rb7] }) (15... Ne7 { Your knight on f5 drops back to guard its partner: safe, but it lets go of d4. } 16. Qc2 Rb8 17. Ra4 { Slightly better for him: his rook comes up to chase your queen, so d4 has to wait while she runs. [%cal Ra4b4] }) (15... O-O { Castling leaves your knight on c6 hanging. } 16. Bxc6 Rb8 17. O-O { Much better for him: his bishop took your knight on c6 for nothing. [%csl Rc6] }) (15... Qxb2 { Grabbing the pawn leaves your knight to his bishop, with check. } 16. Bxc6+ Bd7 17. Bxd7+ Kxd7 { Much better for him: he won a knight for a pawn, and your king had to take back in the middle of the board. [%csl Rd7] }) 16. Bxc6 { He takes your knight. Take back with the bishop. } 16... Bxc6 { Your light bishop, shut in behind your pawns at the start, is out, and his light bishop is gone. Your queen hits d4, the base of his pawn chain, and b2 too. A level game, and a good French. [%cal Gb4d4,Gb4b2] } 0-1


[Event "French Defence: the bishop to d3"]
