[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1b1kbnr/pp3ppp/1qn1p3/2ppP3/3P4/2P2N2/PP3PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_3_6"]

{ Back to the main line: your queen is on b6, hitting his b pawn and backing up your c pawn. What will he do about it? }
6. Bd3 { His bishop comes out to d3 this time, aiming at your king side. Does that change your plan? What now? } 6... cxd4 { Your pawn takes. If his c pawn takes back, only pieces guard d4. If he castles instead, he is giving you a pawn for time. [%csl Gd4] } (6... Nf6 { The king knight comes out too soon: his pawn on e5 takes it. } 7. exf6 gxf6 { Much better for him: his pawn took your knight, and your king side pawns are broken as well. [%csl Rf6] }) (6... Bd7 { Just as good, and the most popular move here: the bishop first, and the trade can wait. } 7. dxc5 Bxc5 { About level: he took on c5, but your bishop took back and stands well. [%csl Gc5] }) (6... Nh6 { Just as good: the knight heads for the edge first. } 7. dxc5 Bxc5 { About level: after the trade on c5 your bishop is active, and your knight is ready to jump. [%csl Gc5] }) (6... a6 { Just as good: a quiet pawn move that keeps his pieces off b5. } 7. O-O cxd4 8. cxd4 { Level: the trade happened a move later, and b5 is covered already. [%csl Ga6] }) (6... Qc7 { The queen steps back, and the pressure on d4 and on his b pawn is gone. } 7. Na3 { Slightly better for him: your queen went backwards, and his knight on a3 eyes b5. [%cal Ra3b5] }) (6... Nge7 { The knight heads out, but it lets him take on c5 first. } 7. dxc5 Qxc5 { Slightly better for him: he took on c5, and your queen had to take back, a step behind. [%csl Rc5] }) (6... Be7 { The bishop comes out quietly, and he gets to take on c5 first. } 7. dxc5 Bxc5 8. O-O { Slightly better for him: he took on c5 and castled, and your bishop had to move twice. [%csl Rc5] }) (6... c4 { Pushing past locks the queen side, but it lets go of d4 for good. } 7. Bc2 Qc7 { Better for him: your c pawn no longer hits d4, and his pieces are free to play on the king side. [%csl Rc4] }) 7. cxd4 { He takes back, and d4 is guarded by pieces only. Grabbing it at once looks tempting. Is it safe? [%csl Rd4] } 7... Bd7 { Your bishop covers b5, so his bishop can no longer check there and uncover his queen. If his knight comes out to c3, the pawn can be taken right away. [%csl Gb5] } (7... Nf6 { The knight heads for f6, but his e pawn still covers that square. } 8. exf6 gxf6 { Lost for you: a whole knight for one pawn, and your king side pawns are split. [%csl Rf6] }) (7... Nxd4 { The knight grabs the pawn at once. It looks free. } 8. Nxd4 Qxd4 9. Bb5+ Bd7 10. Bxd7+ Kxd7 11. Qxd4 { Lost for you: the check on b5 uncovered his queen onto yours, and a pawn is all you got for her. [%cal Rd1d4] }) (7... Bb4+ { A bishop check, but his knight blocks and develops in one move. } 8. Nc3 Bd7 9. Be3 { Slightly better for him: your bishop only helped his knight come out, and his other bishop now guards d4. [%csl Rd4] }) (7... Nge7 { The knight heads out, but d4 is still poisoned: a bishop check on b5 would uncover his queen onto yours. } 8. Nc3 Bd7 9. Be3 { Slightly better for him: his bishop on e3 guards d4, and you have nothing to take. [%cal Re3d4] }) (7... Nb4 { The knight hits his bishop, but the bishop simply steps back. } 8. Be2 { Slightly better for him: his bishop is safe on e2, and your knight on b4 has nothing to hit. [%csl Rb4] }) (7... Nh6 { The knight heads for f5, but his bishop on d3 is waiting to take it there. } 8. Nc3 { Slightly better for him: his knight came out, and d4 is still out of reach. [%csl Rd4] }) (7... Be7 { The bishop comes out, but b5 is still open for his bishop. } 8. Nc3 Bd7 9. Na4 { Slightly better for him: his knight came out and hit your queen, and d4 still cannot be taken. [%cal Ra4b6] }) 8. O-O { He castles instead of guarding: a gambit, a pawn offered for quick development. A move ago it was poisoned. What changed? } 8... Nxd4 { Now it is safe: your bishop covers b5, so no check uncovers his queen. If he trades knights, your queen takes back. [%cal Gd7b5,Gb6d4] } (8... Nf6 { The knight comes out to f6, but his e pawn takes it before you get to the free pawn. } 9. exf6 gxf6 { Lost for you: his pawn took your knight, and one pawn back is not enough. [%csl Rf6] }) (8... Rc8 { Just as good: a useful rook move, though his pawn on d4 stays alive for now. } 9. Be2 { Level: his bishop stepped back, so his queen guards d4 again, and the free pawn is gone. [%cal Rd1d4] }) (8... a6 { Covering b5 again is safe, but slow: the pawn on d4 was free now. [%cal Ga6b5] } 9. Bc2 { About level: you missed a free pawn, and his bishop stepped back to safety. [%csl Rd4] }) (8... Nge7 { The knight comes out, and d4 can wait. } 9. Nc3 Nxd4 10. Nxd4 Qxd4 { About level: you did win the pawn, but his knight came out first, and he has good play for it. [%csl Rc3] }) (8... Nb4 { The knight hits his bishop, but the bishop just steps back, out of reach. } 9. Be2 Bb5 10. Bxb5+ Qxb5 { Slightly better for him: the light bishops came off, and his queen guards d4 again. [%cal Rd1d4] }) (8... Nh6 { The knight heads for f5, but the pawn on d4 was free now. } 9. Nc3 Nxd4 10. Nxd4 Qxd4 { Slightly better for him: you won the pawn a move late, and his knight got out first. [%csl Rh6] }) (8... Be7 { The bishop comes out, but taking the pawn now was better. } 9. Kh1 Nxd4 10. Be3 { About level: you took the pawn a move later, but his bishop now hits your knight on d4. [%cal Re3d4] }) (8... Bb4 { The bishop comes out to b4, but it gives him time to guard d4. [%csl Rd4] } 9. Be3 Nh6 10. Nc3 { Better for him: his bishop on e3 guards d4, and his knight is out. [%cal Re3d4] }) (8... O-O-O { Castling long puts your king where his pieces are coming. } 9. Nc3 Kb8 10. Na4 { Better for him: his knight on a4 hits your queen, and your king faces his attack on the queen side. [%cal Ra4b6] }) 9. Nxd4 { He trades knights, though his other knight to d2 was a little better. Take back with the queen. } 9... Qxd4 { Your queen takes back, a pawn up and right in the middle of the board. Against a queen move or a rook move, just keep developing. [%csl Gd4] } 10. Nc3 { His knight comes out with an idea of its own, and his pawn on e5 looks free. What comes first? } 10... a6 { Your a pawn takes b5 away from his knight. You are a pawn up, and your queen still stands in the middle of the board. [%csl Gb5] } (10... Qxe5 { Grabbing a second pawn looks greedy and good. } 11. Re1 Qd6 12. Nb5 { Level: two pawns up, but his knight jumps to b5 with a hit on your queen, and your king is stuck in the middle. [%cal Rb5d6] }) (10... Qb6 { The queen steps back to safety, but it gives his pieces time. } 11. a3 { Level: still a pawn up, but your queen went back and gave him time. [%csl Gb6] }) (10... Be7 { Developing, but it lets his knight in. } 11. Nb5 Qxe5 { Level: two pawns up, but his knight sits on b5 and your king is still in the middle. [%csl Rb5] }) (10... Ne7 { The knight comes out, but his knight jumps to b5 first. } 11. Nb5 Qxe5 12. Re1 { About level: two pawns up, but his rook hits your queen and his knight eyes c7. [%cal Re1e5,Rb5c7] }) (10... Bb4 { The bishop hits his knight, but the knight just jumps away with a threat. } 11. Nb5 Qxe5 12. Qg4 { Better for him: his queen and knight both attack, and your king is still in the middle. [%cal Rg4g7,Rb5c7] }) (10... Nf6 { The knight comes out onto f6, where his pawn on e5 simply takes it. } 11. exf6 Qxf6 { Much better for him: his pawn took your knight, and a pawn does not pay for it. [%csl Rf6] }) (10... Nh6 { The knight comes out, but it gives his pieces time. } 11. Be3 Qb4 12. a3 { Slightly better for him: his bishop and a pawn chased your queen, even though you are a pawn up. [%cal Ra3b4] }) (10... Bc5 { The bishop comes out, straight into the path of his bishop. } 11. Be3 Qb4 12. a3 { Better for him: his a pawn chases your queen, and his bishop hits yours on c5. [%cal Ra3b4,Re3c5] }) (10... O-O-O { Castling long walks into his dark bishop. } 11. Be3 Qxe5 12. Rc1 { Much better for him: his rook aims at your king through his own knight, and the knight is ready to jump out of the way. [%cal Rc1c8] }) 0-1


[Event "French Defence: the bishop steps back to c2"]
