{ Back to the line with his bishop on d3: your own bishop has just come out to guard b5. [%cal Gd7b5] }
8. Bc2 { His bishop steps back, and now his queen looks straight up the d column at d4. Grabbing the pawn no longer works. What else can you do? [%cal Rd1d4] } 8... Nb4 { Your knight hits his light bishop, the piece aimed at your king side. Against a knight move, take it with check; if it runs to a4, your queen jumps to a6. Now watch. [%cal Gb4c2][%tail] } (8... Nf6 { The knight to f6 walks into his pawn on e5. } 9. exf6 gxf6 { Much better for him: a knight gone for a pawn, and your king side is open. [%csl Rf6] }) (8... Nxd4 { The knight grabs d4, but his queen now guards it from d1. [%cal Rd1d4] } 9. Qxd4 Qxd4 10. Nxd4 { Much better for him: two of his pieces guarded d4 and only one of yours, so you lost a knight for a pawn. [%csl Rd4] }) (8... Qa5+ { A check, but his knight blocks it, and your queen has left b6. } 9. Nc3 Nb4 10. Bb1 { About level: his bishop hid on b1, and the knight trade never happened. [%csl Rb1] }) (8... Rc8 { The rook comes to the c column, but it gives his bishop time. } 9. Nc3 { Slightly better for him: his knight came out first, and his light bishop is still on the board. [%csl Rc2] }) (8... Nge7 { The knight heads out, but his bishop keeps its good diagonal. } 9. Nc3 Na5 { Slightly better for him: his bishop still aims at your king side, and your knight on a5 is far from the action. [%cal Rc2h7] }) (8... Nh6 { The knight heads for f5, where his bishop can take it. } 9. Nc3 Nf5 10. Bxf5 exf5 { Slightly better for him: he traded his bishop for your knight on his own terms, and your f pawns are doubled. [%csl Rf5] }) (8... Bb4+ { The check looks active, but his knight blocks and comes out in the same move. } 9. Nc3 Nge7 10. O-O { Slightly better for him: his knight came out for free, and your bishop on b4 has nothing to do. [%csl Rb4] }) (8... O-O-O { Castling long is bold, but it takes your king toward his pawns. } 9. Nc3 Kb8 10. a3 { Slightly better for him: your king sits on the queen side, right where his pawns can attack. [%csl Rb8] }) (8... Be7 { The bishop comes out, but his light bishop stays alive. } 9. Nc3 O-O-O 10. O-O { Better for him: both his bishops stay on the board, and he castled in peace. [%csl Rc2] }) 9. O-O Nxc2 10. Qxc2 Ne7 { His light bishop is gone, traded for your knight, so you have two bishops against his one. Your last knight is one jump from f5. [%csl Gd7,Gf8] } 0-1


[Event "French Defence: he takes the knight on h6"]
[Result "0-1"]
