{ Back to the main line: your knight has just come to h6, on its way to f5. }
8. Bxh6 { His bishop takes your knight straight away. Taking back with the pawn looks automatic. Is there anything better? [%csl Rh6] } 8... Qxb2 { Your queen grabs his b pawn first and hits his rook in the corner. After a bishop move back, your queen takes that rook; if his bishop takes on g7, take it back with yours. [%cal Gb2a1] } (8... gxh6 { The natural recapture, but it gives him time to guard his b pawn. } 9. Qd2 { Slightly better for him: his queen now guards the b pawn, and your king side pawns are broken. [%csl Rh6] }) (8... Nxd4 { Grabbing d4 first leaves you short of guards there. } 9. Qxd4 Qxd4 10. Nxd4 gxh6 { Much better for him: his queen and knight took back on d4, and you are a knight down for a pawn. [%csl Rd4] }) (8... Bd7 { Developing, but you never take back on h6. } 9. Bc1 { Much better for him: his bishop took your knight and simply went home. [%csl Rc1] }) (8... Qb4+ { A check first, but his bishop blocks it on d2 and is safe. } 9. Bd2 Qxb2 10. Nc3 { Much better for him: the block saved his bishop, and you are a knight down for a pawn. [%csl Rd2] }) (8... Be7 { The bishop comes out, but you never take back on h6, a whole knight down. } 9. Bc1 { Much better for him: the bishop move did nothing, and a whole knight is gone. [%csl Rh6] }) (8... Bb4+ { A bishop check, but his bishop blocks it and gets away. } 9. Bd2 { Much better for him: he blocked with his bishop, and you are simply a knight down. [%csl Rd2] }) 9. Nbd2 { His knight comes out, and now his queen guards the rook in the corner. Take back on h6 now. Had his knight gone to c3, your queen would take it with check. } 9... gxh6 { Bishop and knight are traded, and his b pawn is yours. If his knight comes to b3, check with your bishop on b4. [%csl Gb2] } (9... Nxd4 { Grabbing d4 leaves his bishop on h6 alive. } 10. Nxd4 Qxd4 11. Be3 { Much better for him: his bishop got away with a hit on your queen, and you are a piece down for two pawns. [%cal Re3d4] }) (9... Qa3 { The queen steps away first, but his bishop gets away too. } 10. Bf4 { Much better for him: his bishop walked back to f4, and you are a knight down for a pawn. [%csl Rf4] }) (9... Bd7 { Developing, but you never take back, and his bishop escapes from h6. } 10. Be3 { Much better for him: his bishop is safe on e3, and your knight is simply gone. [%csl Re3] }) (9... Be7 { The bishop comes out, and his bishop on h6 is still alive. } 10. Bxg7 Rg8 11. Bf6 { Much better for him: his bishop took your knight, then the g7 pawn too, and got away. [%csl Rf6] }) 10. O-O { He castles. Your queen is deep in his camp. What next? [%csl Rb2] } 10... Nxd4 { Your knight takes a second pawn and hits his bishop on e2: two pawns up. If his knight takes on d4, your queen takes back, and a bishop check after that is blocked by your bishop. [%cal Gd4e2] } (10... Bd7 { Just as good: develop first, and take the pawn later. } 11. Nb3 { Level: you are a pawn up, and your pieces keep pressing d4. [%csl Gd4] }) (10... a5 { Just as good: your a pawn gains space and gives the queen room. } 11. Nb3 a4 { About level: your a pawn chases his knight, and your queen still has room. [%csl Ga4] }) (10... Qa3 { Just as good: the queen steps back before his rook arrives. } 11. Rb1 Qxa2 12. Nb3 { About level: you grabbed the a pawn too, but your queen is far away on a2. [%csl Ra2] }) (10... Bg7 { The bishop comes out, but the pawn on d4 was worth taking first. } 11. Nb3 Qa3 12. Bb5 { Slightly better for him: his bishop pins your knight to your king, and d4 is well guarded now. [%cal Rb5c6] }) (10... Qb6 { The queen goes home, and his pieces come out. } 11. Rb1 { Slightly better for him: his rook took the b column with a hit on your queen. [%cal Rb1b6] }) (10... Be7 { The bishop comes out, but your queen stays loose on b2. } 11. Nb3 O-O 12. a3 { Slightly better for him: your queen is short of squares, and his a pawn keeps her off a3. [%csl Rb2] }) (10... Bb4 { The bishop hits his knight on d2, but the knight is well guarded. } 11. Nb3 Qa3 12. Ne1 { Slightly better for him: his knights regrouped, and the pawn on d4 is safe now. [%csl Rd4] }) 11. Rb1 { His rook hits your queen, and moving her is the obvious answer. Is it the best one? Had he checked with his queen on a4 instead, your knight would block. [%cal Rb1b2] } 11... Nxe2+ { Your knight takes his bishop with check, so your queen still has time to step away afterwards. } (11... Qc3 { Just as good: the queen steps to c3 and keeps an eye on d4. } 12. Nxd4 Qxd4 { Level: a pair of knights came off on d4, and your queen stands right in the middle. [%csl Gd4] }) (11... Nxf3+ { Taking his knight with check looks natural, but it swaps off your best piece. } 12. Nxf3 Qc3 13. Bb5+ Kd8 14. Qa4 { Better for him: your king had to step to d8 and can never castle, even though you are two pawns up. [%csl Rd8] }) (11... Qxa2 { Grabbing a third pawn leaves your knight on d4 without a guard. } 12. Nxd4 a6 13. Ra1 { Lost for you: three pawns for the knight, but your queen is almost trapped on the edge, and his rook and knights close the net. [%cal Ra1a2] }) 12. Qxe2 { His queen takes back, and your queen is still attacked by his rook. The pawn on a2 looks free. Where should she go? [%cal Rb1b2] } 12... Qa3 { Your queen steps to a3, out of the rook's reach, and guards the dark squares near your king. Against his knight to d4, push your a pawn one step. [%cal Ga3e7] } (12... Qxa2 { Just as good: you grab the a pawn too, though his queen checks and wins a pawn back. } 13. Qb5+ Bd7 14. Qxb7 { About level: his queen checked and took your b pawn, and your queen is far off on a2. [%csl Ra2] }) (12... Qc2 { Just as good: the queen stays active on c2, though his pieces can chase her there. } 13. Nd4 { About level: his knight hits your queen, and his pieces are closer to your king now. [%cal Rd4c2] }) (12... Qc3 { The queen steps to c3, but his rook comes to the c column and chases her again. } 13. Rfc1 Qa3 14. Qb5+ Bd7 15. Qxb7 { Better for him: his rook chased your queen, then his queen checked and took your b pawn. [%csl Rb7] }) (12... Qb6 { The queen steps back to b6, straight into the line of his rook. } 13. Rxb6 axb6 14. Qb5+ Bd7 15. Qxb6 { Lost for you: his rook took your queen, and a rook is far too little for her. [%csl Rb6] }) 13. Qb5+ { Check, and his queen also hits your pawn on b7, though his knight to d4 was a little better. How do you meet the check? [%cal Rb5e8] } 13... Bd7 { The bishop blocks the check, and your king stays at home, still able to castle later. [%cal Gd7b5] } (13... Kd8 { The king steps aside, but it gives up castling and stands in the open. } 14. Nd4 Qa6 15. Qxa6 bxa6 { About level: the queens are off, but your king had to walk and your queen side pawns are split. [%csl Ra6] }) (13... Ke7 { The king steps up to e7, right into the open. } 14. Rfc1 Qa6 15. Qb4+ Ke8 { Slightly better for him: his checks pushed your king back, and his rook owns the c column. [%cal Rc1c8] }) 14. Qxb7 { His queen takes your b pawn and now hits your rook in the corner. Look after it. [%cal Rb7a8] } 14... Rd8 { Level, and a pawn up: his b and d pawns fell, and he got back only yours on b7. Your king side pawns are broken and your king is still in the middle, which is the price. [%csl Gd8] } (14... Rc8 { Just as good: the rook goes to the c column instead. } 15. Rfd1 { Level: still a pawn up, with your rook on the c column and his on the d column. [%csl Gc8] }) (14... Bg7 { The bishop comes out, but your rook in the corner is still hanging. } 15. Qxa8+ { Lost for you: his queen took your rook in the corner, with check. [%csl Ra8] }) (14... Ke7 { Your king steps toward the middle and forgets the rook in the corner. } 15. Qxa8 { Lost for you: his queen took the rook on a8 for free. [%csl Ra8] }) 0-1


