{ Back to the main line once more: your queen is on b6, and your c pawn still hits d4. }
6. a3 { He pushes his a pawn one step, getting ready to push his b pawn and grab space on the queen side. What now? [%cal Rb2b4] } 6... Nh6 { A knight on the edge with a job: from here it can jump to a square that hits d4. If his bishop takes it, your queen grabs his b pawn; against a bishop move to d3, the plan stays the same. [%csl Gd4] } (6... Nf6 { Bringing the knight to f6 forgets his pawn on e5. } 7. exf6 gxf6 { Much better for him: his e pawn took your knight, and a pawn back does not make up for it. [%csl Rf6] }) (6... c4 { Just as good: the c pawn locks the queen side and stops his b pawn from getting anywhere. } 7. Nbd2 Na5 8. Be2 { Level: the queen side is locked, and your knight on a5 eyes b3. [%csl Gb3] }) (6... Nge7 { Just as good: the knight comes out the other way. } 7. dxc5 Qxc5 { About level: he took on c5, and your queen took back with your king knight already out. [%csl Ge7] }) (6... Bd7 { Just as good: the bishop first. } 7. b4 cxd4 8. cxd4 { About level: the same trade on d4, with your bishop already out. [%csl Gd7] }) (6... a5 { Just as good: your a pawn stops his b pawn from coming forward. } 7. Bd3 { About level: his b pawn stays at home, and his bishop comes out instead. [%csl Ga5] }) (6... cxd4 { Trading now is a little early: his knight gets c3. } 7. cxd4 Nh6 8. Nc3 { Slightly better for him: his knight is out on c3, and d4 has plenty of guards. [%csl Rd4] }) (6... f6 { Just as good: hitting the front of his chain holds up here, though it is a little early. } 7. Bd3 fxe5 8. dxe5 { About level: his d pawn now stands on e5, and your pawn on e6 is weak. [%csl Re6] }) (6... Be7 { Also fine, if a little slow: the bishop steps to e7 and waits. } 7. b4 c4 8. a4 { Slightly better for him: his pawns took space on the queen side, and your bishop is only just out. [%csl Rb4] }) 7. b4 { His b pawn jumps forward and hits your pawn on c5. If that pawn falls, d4 is safe for good. What now? [%cal Rb4c5] } 7... cxd4 { You trade before his pawn can take yours. When his c pawn takes back, d4 is the base again. If his bishop takes on h6 first, take back. [%csl Gd4] } (7... c4 { Locking the queen side now leaves his b pawn well placed. } 8. a4 { Slightly better for him: his pawns roll on the queen side, and d4 is safe for good. [%csl Rd4] }) (7... cxb4 { Taking on b4 gives his rook the a column. } 8. axb4 { Better for him: his rook owns the a column, and your queen side is weak. [%cal Ra1a7] }) (7... Nf5 { The knight jumps out, but your c pawn is taken first. } 8. bxc5 { Better for him: his pawn took yours and now hits your queen. [%cal Rc5b6] }) (7... Qc7 { The queen steps back, and his bishop takes your knight. } 8. Bxh6 gxh6 9. bxc5 { Better for him: your king side pawns are wrecked, and he won your c pawn too. [%csl Rh6] }) (7... Be7 { The bishop comes out, but his bishop can take your knight on h6 and his pawns can take on c5. } 8. Bxh6 gxh6 9. dxc5 { Better for him: he took on h6 and on c5, and you are a pawn down with broken pawns. [%csl Rc5] }) (7... Bd7 { Developing, but your c pawn is left to be taken. } 8. bxc5 Qd8 9. Bxh6 gxh6 { Better for him: he won your c pawn, and your king side pawns are broken. [%csl Rc5] }) 8. cxd4 { He takes back. Something of yours is in danger again. What now? } 8... Nf5 { The knight jumps out of danger and hits d4, together with your queen and your other knight. If his bishop comes to e3 to guard it, your knight can take it. [%cal Gf5d4] } (8... Bd7 { Just as good: the bishop comes out first, and the knight jumps next move. } 9. Nc3 Nf5 10. Na4 { About level: your knight reached f5 a move later, and his knight on a4 hits your queen. [%cal Ra4b6] }) (8... a6 { A quiet pawn move, and his bishop takes your knight. } 9. Bxh6 gxh6 { Slightly better for him: your king side pawns are split in two. [%csl Rh6] }) (8... Qc7 { The queen leaves b6, and his bishop can still take your knight on h6. } 9. Bxh6 gxh6 10. Bd3 { Slightly better for him: his bishop took your knight, and his other bishop now points at your broken king side. [%cal Rd3h7] }) (8... Be7 { The bishop comes out and leaves your knight to his bishop. } 9. Bxh6 gxh6 10. Nc3 { Better for him: your king side is wrecked for good, and his pieces come out fast. [%csl Rg7] }) (8... g5 { The g pawn steps in front of your knight to block his bishop, but the bishop simply takes it. } 9. Bxg5 { Better for him: his bishop took your g pawn, and your king side is full of holes. [%csl Rg5] }) (8... Bxb4+ { Grabbing his b pawn with check looks free, but your bishop is simply taken. } 9. axb4 Nf5 10. b5 { Much better for him: his a pawn took your bishop, and one pawn does not pay for it. [%csl Rb4] }) 9. Bb2 { His bishop guards d4 from b2. How do you go on? [%cal Rb2d4] } 9... Bd7 { The bishop comes out and makes room for your rooks. If his bishop comes to e2 instead, bring your dark bishop to e7. } (9... a6 { Just as good: a pawn step that keeps his pieces off b5. } 10. g4 Nfe7 11. Nc3 { Level: his g pawn chased your knight back, but b5 is covered. [%csl Ga6] }) (9... Be7 { Just as good: the dark bishop comes out first. } 10. h4 { About level: his h pawn is on the march, but your pieces stand well. [%csl Rh4] }) (9... Qd8 { The queen goes home, and the pressure on d4 drops. } 10. Bd3 Bd7 11. O-O { About level, but your queen went back, and he castled in peace. [%csl Rd8] }) (9... h6 { A pawn move that does nothing for d4. } 10. h4 { About level, but his h pawn gained space next to your knight. [%csl Rh4] }) (9... Bxb4+ { Taking the b pawn with check looks tempting. } 10. axb4 Qxb4+ 11. Bc3 { Much better for him: you got two pawns for your bishop, and his rook owns the a column. [%cal Ra1a7] }) 10. g4 { His g pawn charges at your knight. The pawn on d4 looks ripe for the taking. Is it? [%cal Rg4f5] } 10... Nfe7 { The knight steps back safely. Your queen and your other knight still point at d4, and his pawns rushed up on both wings, leaving holes behind them. [%csl Gd4] } (10... Nh6 { Just as good, and the most popular move: the knight goes back to the edge. } 11. Rg1 { Level: your knight is safe on h6, and his rook comes to the g column. [%csl Rg1] }) (10... Nfxd4 { Taking d4 looks natural, but his pieces guard it well. } 11. Bxd4 Nxd4 12. Qxd4 { Better for him: you gave two knights for his bishop and a pawn, and his queen stands in the middle. [%csl Rd4] }) (10... Ncxd4 { The other knight takes, but that leaves your knight on f5 to his pawn. } 11. gxf5 Nxf5 12. Bd3 { Better for him: his pawn took your knight, and two pawns are not enough for it. [%csl Rf5] }) (10... a5 { Hitting his b pawn ignores the pawn hitting your knight. } 11. gxf5 axb4 12. a4 { Better for him: he took your knight, and a pawn is all you have for it. [%csl Rf5] }) (10... Bxb4+ { A check first, grabbing a pawn, but his a pawn takes your bishop. } 11. axb4 Nfe7 12. b5 { Much better for him: you gave a bishop for one pawn, and your knight still had to run. [%csl Rb4] }) 0-1


