{ Back to the pawn chain: your c pawn has just hit the base of his chain. }
4. Nf3 { He brings his knight out first and leaves his c pawn at home. What changes for you? } 4... cxd4 { You trade before his c pawn can prop up the base. If his queen takes back, or a bishop move comes first, just bring your knights out. [%csl Gd4] } (4... Qa5+ { A queen check, but it only asks him to play a move he wanted anyway. } 5. c3 { About level: his c pawn blocked the check and now props up d4, and your queen stands on the edge. [%csl Ra5] }) (4... Nc6 { Also fine: after his c pawn comes to c3, you are back in the main line. } 5. c3 Qb6 { Level: the same position as the main line, reached through a side door. [%csl Gb6] }) (4... Qb6 { Also fine: the queen lines up behind your c pawn and hits b2. } 5. Bd3 cxd4 6. O-O { Level: you won his d pawn, but he castled fast and his pieces lead the way. [%csl Gd4] }) (4... Bd7 { Just as good: the bishop first, a quiet plan. } 5. c3 Nc6 { Level: a quiet road into the main line, with your bishop already on d7. [%csl Bd7] }) (4... Ne7 { The king knight comes out, a little slower. } 5. c3 Nbc6 6. Na3 { About level: his c pawn props up the base after all, and your chance to hit it early has passed. [%csl Rc3] }) (4... Nf6 { The knight comes out where his pawn on e5 can take it. } 5. exf6 gxf6 { Much better for him: you gave a knight for a pawn, and your king side is broken. [%csl Rf6] }) (4... Nh6 { The knight goes to the edge, where his bishop can take it. } 5. Bxh6 gxh6 { About level: his bishop took the knight on h6, and your pawns on the h column are doubled now. [%csl Rh6] }) (4... Be7 { The dark bishop comes out to e7, a little passive here. } 5. c3 Bd7 6. Bd3 { About level: your bishop on e7 hits nothing, and his c pawn now props up d4. [%csl Re7] }) (4... Nd7 { The queen knight goes to d7, where it hits his pawn on e5 but blocks your bishop. } 5. c3 Ne7 6. Bd3 { About level: your knight on d7 shuts in your light bishop, and his pawns stand firm. [%csl Rd7] }) (4... c4 { Pushing past takes the pressure off the base of his chain. } 5. b3 cxb3 6. axb3 { Better for him: your c pawn no longer hits the base of his chain, and his rook looks straight at your pawn on a7. [%cal Ra1a7] }) (4... Qc7 { The queen hits his pawn on e5 at once. } 5. c3 Nc6 6. Na3 { About level: his pawns stand firm, and his pawn on e5 is well guarded. [%csl Re5] }) 5. Nxd4 { His knight takes back and stands proudly in the middle. What now? } 5... Nc6 { Your knight hits his knight in the middle and his pawn on e5 at the same time. If he only guards the knight, that pawn falls. [%cal Gc6d4,Gc6e5] } (5... Nh6 { The knight heads for f5 by the edge, but here nothing stops his bishop from taking it. } 6. Bd3 Nc6 7. Nxc6 bxc6 8. Bxh6 gxh6 { About level: his bishop took your knight on h6, and your king side pawns are split. [%csl Rh6] }) (5... Ne7 { Just as good: the king knight comes first. } 6. Bd3 Nbc6 7. Nxc6 Nxc6 { Level: one pair of knights came off, and your other knight took back in the middle. [%csl Gc6] }) (5... Qc7 { Also fine: the queen hits his pawn on e5 at once. } 6. f4 Nc6 7. Be3 { Level: his f pawn guards e5, and the game goes on quietly. [%csl Be5] }) (5... Bd7 { Also fine: a quiet developing move. } 6. Bd3 Ne7 7. Nd2 { Level: both sides bring their pieces out, and his knight still stands in the middle. [%csl Bd4] }) (5... Bc5 { Also playable: the bishop hits his knight, though your king side loses its dark guard. } 6. Qg4 g6 7. Nc3 { About level: his queen hit your pawn on g7, and pushing it to g6 left holes around your king. [%csl Rg6] }) (5... Qb6 { Just as good: the queen hits his knight in the middle and his pawn on b2. } 6. Be3 Qxb2 7. Nd2 { About level: you grabbed a pawn, but his pieces come out fast while your queen is far from home. [%csl Rb2] }) (5... Nf6 { Developing the knight to f6 walks into his e pawn. } 6. exf6 Qxf6 7. Bb5+ { Much better for him: a knight for a pawn, and now his bishop checks your king. [%cal Rb5e8] }) (5... Bb4+ { A check, but his c pawn blocks it and hits your bishop. } 6. c3 { Slightly better for him: your bishop has to step back, and his c pawn now guards his knight. [%csl Rb4] }) (5... Be7 { The dark bishop goes to e7, where it only guards. } 6. Bd3 { Slightly better for him: your bishop on e7 does little, and his bishop aims at your king side. [%csl Re7] }) (5... Nd7 { The queen knight goes to d7 and blocks your light bishop. } 6. f4 Ne7 7. Be3 { Slightly better for him: his f pawn props up e5, and your knights get in each other's way. [%csl Rd7] }) (5... Qa5+ { A check, but his knight blocks and comes out. } 6. Nc3 Nc6 7. Nb3 { About level: his knight dropped back to b3 with a hit on your queen, and he develops with every move. [%cal Rb3a5] }) 6. Nxc6 { He trades knights. Take back with your b pawn, the only one that can. Against a bishop move to b5 instead, you would unpin with your own bishop; against a knight move back, keep developing. } 6... bxc6 { Your pawn takes back toward the middle, and now your c and e pawns both guard your d pawn. Against a knight move or a bishop move next, simply develop too. [%cal Gc6d5,Ge6d5] } (6... Bd7 { The bishop comes out, but his knight on c6 hits your queen. } 7. Nxd8 Rxd8 { Lost for you: his knight took your queen, and you won only the knight back. [%csl Rd8] }) (6... Qc7 { The queen hits his pawn on e5 instead of taking back. } 7. Nd4 Qxe5+ 8. Be3 { Much better for him: his knight escaped, and one pawn does not pay for it. [%csl Rd4] }) 7. Bd3 { His bishop comes out and aims at your king side. Many quiet moves keep the game level here. Which do you choose? } 7... Ba6 { Your light bishop, shut in behind your pawns since the start, offers itself for his bishop on d3. [%cal Ga6d3] } (7... Nh6 { The knight goes to the edge, and his bishop is ready to take it. } 8. Bxh6 gxh6 9. O-O { About level: his bishop took the knight and broke your king side pawns, and his king is castled. [%csl Rh6] }) (7... Bb7 { The bishop goes to b7, but there it only looks at your own pawn on c6. } 8. O-O a5 9. Nd2 { About level: your bishop on b7 bites on your own pawn, and his king is already castled. [%csl Rb7] }) (7... Qc7 { Just as good: the queen hits his pawn on e5. } 8. Qe2 Ne7 9. O-O { Level: his queen guards e5, and both sides are nearly ready. [%csl Be5] }) (7... Ne7 { Just as good: the king knight comes out. } 8. O-O Qb6 9. c4 { Level: his c pawn hits your pawn on d5, and the fight moves to the middle. [%cal Rc4d5] }) (7... Bc5 { Just as good: the dark bishop comes out to an open diagonal. } 8. O-O Ne7 9. Nd2 { Level: both sides are nearly developed, and your bishop stands well on c5. [%csl Gc5] }) (7... a5 { Just as good: a useful waiting move. } 8. O-O Ba6 { Level: your light bishop reaches a6 anyway, one move later. [%cal Ga6d3] }) (7... Qa5+ { Just as good: a check that makes him block. } 8. c3 { Level: his pawn on c3 blocks the check and keeps your queen busy. [%csl Rc3] }) (7... c5 { Your c pawn goes forward, and his c pawn hits back at once. } 8. c4 dxc4 9. Qf3 { About level, but his queen hits your rook in the corner along the long diagonal. [%cal Rf3a8] }) (7... Nf6 { The king knight to f6 lands where his e pawn can take it. } 8. exf6 Qxf6 9. O-O { Much better for him: you gave a knight for a pawn and got nothing for it. [%csl Rf6] }) (7... Bb4+ { A check, but his c pawn blocks and pushes your bishop around. } 8. c3 Bc5 9. O-O { About level: your bishop has moved twice, and he castled in the meantime. [%csl Rc5] }) (7... Qb6 { Just as good: the queen hits his pawn on b2. } 8. Qe2 Ne7 9. Nd2 { Level: his bishop on c1 still guards b2, and both sides keep developing. [%cal Rc1b2] }) (7... Bd7 { The bishop comes to d7, a modest square. } 8. Nd2 Ne7 9. O-O { About level: your bishop on d7 has nothing to do, and he castled. [%csl Rd7] }) (7... Be7 { The dark bishop goes to e7, where it only guards the king side. } 8. O-O { Slightly better for him: he castled, and your dark bishop is stuck on guard duty. [%csl Re7] }) (7... d4 { Your d pawn pushes forward and leaves the middle to his pieces. } 8. Nd2 Ne7 9. O-O { Better for him: your pawn on d4 is far from home, and the square e4 is free for his pieces. [%csl Rd4] }) 8. Bxa6 { He takes your bishop at once; castling first was a little better for him. Nothing of yours can take back on a6. Is the bishop simply gone? [%csl Ra6] } 8... Qa5+ { Check, and your queen also hits his bishop on a6. He must answer the check first, so the bishop comes back to you. [%cal Ga5e1,Ga5a6] } (8... Ne7 { Developing the knight forgets that your bishop is gone. } 9. O-O Qc7 { Lost for you: nothing took back on a6, and he is a whole bishop up. [%csl Ra6] }) (8... Bb4+ { A check, but his pawn blocks it, and the bishop on a6 stays his. } 9. c3 Bc5 { Lost for you: his pawn blocked the check, and he is still a bishop up. [%csl Ra6] }) (8... Bc5 { The dark bishop comes out, but it cannot reach a6. } 9. Qe2 Ne7 { Lost for you: he keeps your bishop for nothing, and his queen guards his own bishop. [%cal Re2a6] }) (8... c5 { The pawn goes forward, and his bishop on a6 simply stays. } 9. Bb5+ Ke7 { Lost for you: his bishop checks from b5, and your king has to walk. [%csl Re7] }) (8... Be7 { The bishop steps to e7, and his bishop on a6 stays where it is. } 9. O-O { Lost for you: he is a whole bishop up, with nothing to fear. [%csl Ra6] }) 9. Nc3 { He blocks with his knight. A bishop move to d2 would block too, and his bishop on a6 would still fall. } 9... Qxa6 { The light bishops are traded: yours, shut in behind your pawns, for his active one. Your queen on a6 also watches f1, so he cannot castle on the king side for now. [%cal Ga6f1] } (9... Qxc3+ { Taking the knight with check looks greedy, and his pawn takes your queen. } 10. bxc3 { Lost for you: you gave your queen for a knight. [%csl Rc3] }) (9... Ne7 { Developing now lets his bishop run away. } 10. Be2 { Lost for you: his bishop escaped, and he is a piece up. [%csl Re2] }) (9... Bb4 { The bishop pins his knight, but his bishop on a6 walks away. } 10. Be2 Bxc3+ 11. bxc3 { Much better for him: his bishop got back to e2, and you are a piece down. [%csl Re2] }) (9... Be7 { The bishop steps to e7 and lets his bishop slip away. } 10. Bb7 { Lost for you: his bishop ran to b7, and he stays a piece up. [%csl Rb7] }) 0-1


