k the open column first, but your king is safe. [%csl Re1] }) (6... Nc6 { Also fine: the knight comes out, though your king stays on the open column a move longer. } 7. Re1+ Be6 8. c4 O-O { About level: the check pushed your bishop to e6 in the way, and his c pawn now hits d5. [%cal Re1e6] }) (6... Bg4 { The pin looks tempting, but your king still stands on the open column. } 7. Re1+ Be7 8. h3 Bxf3 9. Qxf3 { Slightly better for him: his check drove your bishop back to e7, and you gave your light bishop for his knight. [%cal Re1e8] }) (6... c5 { Hitting his d pawn now leaves your king on the open column. } 7. Re1+ Be6 8. Ng5 { Slightly better for him: his rook pins your bishop on e6 to your king along the open column, and his knight hits it again. [%cal Re1e8,Rg5e6] }) 7. Bg5 { His bishop pins your knight to your queen. Answer the same way: your light bishop pins his knight. [%cal Rg5d8,Yc8g4] } 7... Bg4 { Now both sides have a pin, and the pawns are still the same. If his pawn comes to h3, your bishop steps back to h5. [%cal Gg4d1] } (7... Be6 { Just as good: the bishop guards d5 instead of pinning. [%cal Ge6d5] } 8. Re1 h6 9. Bh4 { Level: his rook took the open column, and your bishop guards d5. [%cal Ge6d5] }) (7... h6 { Just as good: ask his bishop what it wants. } 8. Bh4 Be6 9. Nc3 { Level: his bishop kept the pin from h4, and only your queen knight is still at home. [%cal Rh4d8] }) (7... c6 { Just as good: a pawn to guard d5 first. [%cal Gc6d5] } 8. Nbd2 Be6 9. Ne5 { Level: his knight jumped forward, and your pawns hold the middle. [%csl Re5] }) (7... Nbd7 { Just as good: the queen knight comes out and guards the knight on f6. } 8. c3 c5 9. Nbd2 { Level: your c pawn hits d4, and your knights guard each other. [%cal Gc5d4] }) (7... Nc6 { Just as good: the queen knight goes to c6. } 8. c3 h6 9. Bh4 { Level: his c pawn guards d4, and his bishop keeps its pin from h4. [%cal Rh4d8,Rc3d4] }) 8. Nbd2 { His queen knight comes out. Against a pawn to c3 instead, the answer is the same: bring your queen knight out too. [%cal Yb8d7] } 8... Nbd7 { Your knight comes out to guard its partner. Against a pawn to h3 or c4, the set-up stays the same. [%cal Gd7f6] } (8... h6 { Just as good: ask his bishop first. } 9. Bh4 Nbd7 10. c4 { Level: the same set-up, and his bishop still pins from h4. [%cal Rh4d8] }) (8... c5 { Just as good: your c pawn goes two squares and hits d4. } 9. c4 Nc6 { Level: both sides push a c pawn, and the middle opens up. [%csl Gc5] }) (8... c6 { Just as good: a pawn to c6 guards d5. [%cal Gc6d5] } 9. h3 Bxf3 10. Qxf3 { Level: you gave your bishop for his knight, and your pawns stand firm. [%csl Gc6] }) (8... Re8 { Just as good: your rook takes the open e column. } 9. h3 Bh5 10. c3 { Level: your rook owns the open column for now. [%csl Ge8] }) (8... Nc6 { Also playable: the queen knight goes to c6 instead of d7. } 9. c3 h6 10. Bh4 { About level: his c pawn blocks your knight's jumps, and his bishop keeps its pin. [%csl Rc3] }) (8... Bh5 { Just as good: the bishop steps back to h5 and keeps its pin. } 9. Re1 c5 { Level: his rook took the open column, and your c pawn hits d4. [%cal Gc5d4] }) 9. c3 { He props up his d pawn with his c pawn. Do the same: your c pawn to c6. [%cal Yc7c6] } 9... c6 { Both kings are castled, every minor piece is out, and the pawns are the same on both sides. Level: the open e column lies empty between the armies, and neither rook has reached it yet. [%cal Ge8e1] } (9... h6 { Just as good: a little air for your king first. } 10. Bh4 c6 11. Re1 { Level: his rook came to the open column first, and your pawn on c6 came a move later. [%csl Re1] }) (9... Re8 { Just as good: the rook grabs the open column right away. } 10. h3 Bxf3 11. Nxf3 { Level: your rook sits on the open column, and he took back on f3 with the knight. [%csl Ge8] }) (9... c5 { Just as good: the c pawn goes two squares instead. } 10. Qc2 Qc7 { Level: both queens come out, and your c pawn hits d4. [%cal Gc5d4] }) (9... Qe8 { The queen goes to the open column, but she blocks your own rook. } 10. h3 Bh5 11. Qc2 { About level: your queen sits where a rook belongs. [%csl Re8] }) (9... Bh5 { Just as good: the bishop drops back to h5 before he asks. } 10. Qc2 h6 { Level: his queen stepped off to c2, and your pawn on h6 gives your king air. [%csl Gh6] }) 0-1


