{ Back to the exchange variation, with the open e column between you. This game's new idea is the isolated pawn: a pawn with no pawn of its own on either column beside it, so no pawn can ever guard it. }
4. c4 { His c pawn hits your pawn on d5. Bring your king knight out to guard it. [%cal Rc4d5,Yg8f6] } 4... Nf6 { Your knight guards d5. If he takes on d5, take back with this knight. [%cal Gf6d5] } (4... Bb4+ { Just as good: the check first, and the knight follows. } 5. Nc3 Nf6 6. Nf3 { Level: your pieces reach the same squares, only in another order. [%csl Gb4] }) (4... Nc6 { Just as good: the queen knight hits d4 at once. } 5. Nc3 { Level: both queen knights are out, and the fight is about d4 and d5. [%csl Gd4] }) (4... Be7 { Just as good: the dark bishop steps to e7. } 5. Nc3 Nf6 6. Nf3 { Level: a calm set-up, with your bishop on e7 guarding the king side. [%csl Ge7] }) (4... c6 { Just as good: a pawn guards d5. [%cal Gc6d5] } 5. Nc3 Nf6 6. Nf3 { Level: your pawn on c6 holds the middle firmly. [%csl Gc6] }) (4... dxc4 { Taking at once lets his bishop take back with a gain of time. } 5. Bxc4 Bd6 6. Nf3 { Level: his bishop came out while taking back, and his d pawn is already isolated. [%csl Gd4] }) (4... Be6 { Just as good: the light bishop comes out first and guards the d pawn. [%cal Ge6d5] } 5. Nc3 Nf6 { Level: your bishop and knight both guard d5 now. [%cal Ge6d5,Gf6d5] }) 5. Nc3 { His knight hits d5 too. Pin it with your dark bishop, which would give check against a knight move to f3 instead. [%cal Rc3d5,Yf8b4] } 5... Bb4 { Your bishop pins his knight to his king. Against a bishop move, you simply castle; if his pawn comes to a3, take the knight first. [%cal Gb4e1] } (5... Nc6 { Just as good: the queen knight joins the fight. } 6. Nf3 { Level: your knight hits d4, and his knight guards it. [%cal Gc6d4,Rf3d4] }) (5... Be7 { Just as good: the bishop goes to e7 and stays safe. } 6. Nf3 O-O 7. Be3 { Level: you castled, and your pawn on d5 is well guarded. [%csl Gd5] }) (5... c6 { Just as good: a pawn guards d5 as well. [%cal Gc6d5] } 6. Bd3 dxc4 7. Bxc4 { Level: his bishop took back on c4, and his d pawn is isolated now. [%csl Gd4] }) (5... Be6 { Just as good: the light bishop guards d5. [%cal Ge6d5] } 6. Nge2 { Level: your bishop guards d5, and his knight comes out to e2. [%cal Ge6d5] }) (5... dxc4 { Just as good: take on c4 at once, and his bishop takes back. } 6. Bxc4 Bd6 7. Nf3 { Level: his bishop took back and came out with the same move, and his d pawn stands alone. [%csl Gd4] }) (5... h6 { Just as good: a quiet pawn move first, so his bishop never pins on g5. } 6. Nf3 Bb4 { Level: your bishop still comes to b4 and pins his knight. [%cal Gb4e1] }) 6. Nf3 { His king knight comes out and guards d4. Castle now: your king leaves the open e column. [%cal Rf3d4,Ye8g8] } 6... O-O { Your king is safe, off the open column. If his light bishop comes out, even to d3, take on c4 next: it must move again to take back. [%csl Gg8] } (6... Bg4 { Just as good: pin his knight first, and castle next. } 7. Be2 dxc4 8. Bxc4 { Level: his bishop took back on c4, and your king can still castle. [%csl Gc4] }) (6... Nc6 { Just as good: the queen knight comes to c6 before you castle. } 7. Bg5 Bg4 8. Be2 { Level: his bishop pins your knight, and your king can still castle. [%cal Rg5d8] }) (6... Be6 { Just as good: your bishop on e6 guards d5 and waits. [%cal Ge6d5] } 7. c5 O-O 8. Bd3 { Level: his c pawn jumped to c5 and grabbed space on the queen side. [%csl Rc5] }) (6... Bxc3+ { Also fine: take his knight at once and castle next. } 7. bxc3 O-O 8. Be2 { Level: his pawns on c3 and c4 are doubled, but he has both bishops. [%csl Rc3,Rc4] }) (6... Ne4 { The knight jumps forward, but his queen comes to b3 and hits your loose bishop on b4. [%cal Rb3b4] } 7. Qb3 Bxc3+ 8. bxc3 { Slightly better for him: you had to give your bishop for his knight, and his queen now hits your pawn on b7. [%cal Rb3b7] }) (6... c5 { Just as good: your c pawn hits d4 and makes it a real fight. } 7. cxd5 O-O 8. Bc4 { Level: he grabbed a pawn on d5, and your quick pieces make up for it. [%csl Rd5] }) (6... h6 { Just as good: a small pawn move before castling. } 7. Bd3 dxc4 8. Bxc4 { Level: the trade on c4 came anyway, and his d pawn stands isolated. [%csl Gd4] }) 7. Be2 { His bishop comes out to e2. Now take on c4: his bishop must move again to take back. [%cal Yd5c4] } 7... dxc4 { Your pawn takes, and his bishop has to spend a second move taking back. If he castles instead, take his knight on c3 and hold the pawn with your b pawn. [%csl Gc4] } (7... Nc6 { Just as good: the knight first, and the trade on c4 comes next. } 8. O-O dxc4 9. Bxc4 { Level: the same trade a move later, with his king castled. [%csl Gc4] }) (7... Bg4 { Just as good: the bishop hits his knight right away. } 8. cxd5 Nxd5 9. Bd2 { Level: he took on d5, and your knight took back in the middle. [%csl Gd5] }) (7... Re8 { Just as good: the rook steps onto the open column. } 8. O-O Be6 9. c5 { Level: his c pawn went on to c5 instead of taking, and your rook holds the open column. [%csl Ge8] }) (7... Ne4 { Just as good: the knight jumps in, and his queen comes to b3. } 8. Qb3 Bxc3+ 9. bxc3 { Level: you gave your bishop for his knight, and his c pawns are doubled. [%csl Rc3,Rc4] }) (7... Be6 { Just as good: the light bishop comes to e6 before any trade. } 8. c5 b6 { Level: his c pawn rushed to c5, and your b pawn hits it right away. [%cal Gb6c5] }) (7... c5 { Just as good: the c pawn hits d4 right now. } 8. O-O dxc4 { Level: you took on c4, and the middle is wide open. [%csl Gc4] }) 8. Bxc4 { His bishop takes back. Now his d pawn is an isolated pawn: no pawn of his stands on the c or e column to guard it. Pin its guard: your bishop to g4. [%csl Rd4][%cal Yc8g4] } 8... Bg4 { Your bishop pins his knight, the piece that guards d4, to his queen. Against a bishop move instead of castling, bring your queen knight out all the same. [%cal Gg4d1,Rf3d4] } (8... Nc6 { Just as good: the knight hits the isolated pawn first. } 9. O-O Bg4 10. Be3 { Level: your knight and bishop both work against d4, and his bishop guards it from e3. [%cal Gc6d4,Re3d4] }) (8... Re8+ { Just as good: check along the open column. } 9. Be3 Be6 10. Bxe6 Rxe6 { Level: the light bishops came off, and your rook stands on the open column. [%csl Ge6] }) (8... Qe7+ { Also fine: the queen checks along the open column. } 9. Be3 Be6 10. Bd3 { Level: his bishop blocked the check, and both sides are nearly developed. [%csl Re3] }) (8... c5 { Just as good: your c pawn hits the isolated pawn from the side. } 9. O-O Nc6 { Level: you hit d4 from the side, but trading pawns would leave him with no weak pawn at all. [%cal Gc5d4] }) (8... c6 { Just as good: a solid pawn move before the pin. } 9. O-O Bg4 { Level: your bishop pins his knight a move later. [%cal Gg4d1] }) 9. O-O { He castles, and his king leaves the open column. Bring your queen knight out to hit d4 as well. [%cal Yb8c6] } 9... Nc6 { His d pawn stands isolated: no pawn can ever guard it. Your bishop pins its guard on f3, and your knight hits d4. Level, with a clear target for you. [%cal Gc6d4,Gg4d1] } (9... Bxf3 { Just as good: take the guard off at once. } 10. Qxf3 Qxd4 { Level: the isolated pawn fell, but his queen and bishop are very active now. [%csl Gd4] }) (9... c6 { Just as good: a quiet pawn to c6 keeps his pieces out of d5. [%cal Gc6d5] } 10. Bg5 h6 11. Bh4 { Level: his bishop pins your knight from h4, and your pawn keeps d5 covered. [%cal Rh4d8,Gc6d5] }) (9... Nbd7 { Just as good: the queen knight comes to d7 instead. } 10. h3 Bh5 11. Re1 { Level: his rook took the open e column, and your bishop stepped back to h5. [%csl Re1] }) (9... Bh5 { Just as good: the bishop steps back to h5 at once. } 10. Qb3 a5 { Level: his queen hit your bishop on b4, and your a pawn guards it. [%cal Ga5b4] }) (9... Qd7 { Just as good: the queen steps up to d7. } 10. h3 Bh5 { Level: his pawn kicked your bishop, and it keeps the pin from h5. [%cal Gh5d1] }) 0-1


