{ Back to the French: your pawns stand on e6 and d5, and your d pawn hits his king pawn. }
3. Nc3 { His knight guards the pawn. Take it anyway: your d pawn swaps itself for his king pawn and opens the road for your pieces. Against his knight to d2, you do the same. [%cal Yd5e4] } 3... dxe4 { You swap your d pawn for his king pawn, and the middle opens for your pieces. If he offers another pawn with his f pawn to f3, take that one too. [%csl Ge4] } (3... Nf6 { Just as good: the knight hits his king pawn, a different French with a study of its own. } 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 { About level: his pawns storm forward, and that sharp fight is for another day. [%csl Bf4] }) (3... Bb4 { Just as good: the bishop pins his knight, the sharpest French of all. } 4. e5 Ne7 5. a3 { About level: his pawn chases your bishop, and the game turns wild. That is another study. [%cal Ra3b4] }) (3... c6 { Also playable: the c pawn backs up your d pawn, though your queen knight loses its best square. } 4. Bd3 dxe4 5. Nxe4 { Slightly better for him: his bishop and knight are out, and your pawn on c6 cost you a move. [%csl Rc6] }) 4. Nxe4 { His knight takes back and stands in the middle. Bring your queen knight to d7: it gets ready to back up your other knight on f6. [%cal Yb8d7] } 4... Nd7 { This knight guards f6, so your other knight can go there, and if he trades, you take back with a knight. Against a bishop move first, the plan is the same. [%csl Gf6] } (4... Bd7 { Just as good: the bishop heads for c6, to fight for the long diagonal. [%cal Gc6f3] } 5. Bd3 Bc6 6. Nf3 { About level: your bishop on c6 faces his knight, another good way to play. [%cal Gc6e4] }) (4... Nf6 { Also fine: the king knight comes out at once, but if he trades, a pawn has to take back. } 5. Nxf6+ gxf6 6. Nf3 { About level: your king side pawns are split, but your rook gets the g column. [%csl Rf6] }) (4... Be7 { Just as good: the bishop comes out first. } 5. Nf3 Nf6 6. Nxf6+ Bxf6 { About level: one pair of knights came off, and your bishop took back and eyes his d pawn. [%cal Gf6d4] }) (4... Nc6 { The queen knight goes to c6, where it blocks your c pawn. } 5. Nf3 Nf6 6. Bd3 { Slightly better for him: your c pawn can no longer strike at his d pawn, and his pieces are out. [%csl Rc6] }) 5. Nf3 { His king knight comes out. Now your king knight goes to f6 to challenge his knight in the middle. [%cal Yg8f6] } 5... Ngf6 { Your knight challenges his. If his bishop comes to d3 instead, take his knight, then hit the bishop with your other knight: back to the main line. [%cal Gf6e4] } (5... Ndf6 { Just as good: the queen knight goes to f6 instead. } 6. Ng3 c5 7. Bc4 { About level: a different road, and your c pawn has already struck at his d pawn. [%cal Gc5d4] }) (5... Be7 { Just as good: the bishop first, and the knight next. } 6. Bf4 Ngf6 7. Nxf6+ Bxf6 { About level: the same knight trade, with your bishop taking back instead of a knight. [%csl Gf6] }) (5... Bd6 { The bishop steps where his knight can take it. } 6. Bd3 Ngf6 7. Nxd6+ cxd6 { Slightly better for him: his knight took your bishop, and your pawn on d6 is a target. [%csl Rd6] }) (5... c5 { The right pawn, but too early: the queens come off, and your king loses the right to castle. } 6. dxc5 Nxc5 7. Qxd8+ Kxd8 { Better for him: your king is stuck in the middle, and his knight hits yours on c5. [%cal Re4c5] }) 6. Nxf6+ { He trades knights, with check. Against a pin with his bishop to g5 you would unpin with your bishop, and against his knight to g3 your c pawn would strike at once. Now take back with the knight. [%cal Yd7f6] } 6... Nxf6 { Your knight takes back, and your pawns stay whole. If his bishop pins it from g5, or his pawn comes to c3, your c pawn still goes two squares. [%csl Gf6] } (6... gxf6 { Taking with the pawn splits your king side pawns. } 7. Bd3 c5 8. Qe2 { Slightly better for him: your pawns are split, and your king has no cosy home on the king side. [%csl Rf6] }) (6... Qxf6 { The queen takes back, but out there she is easy to chase. } 7. Bg5 Qf5 8. Bd3 { Better for him: his bishops chased your queen twice, and both your bishops are still at home. [%cal Rd3f5] }) 7. Bd3 { His bishop comes out and aims at your king side. Now strike at his last pawn in the middle: your c pawn two squares. [%cal Yc7c5] } 7... c5 { That is a pawn break: a pawn push that hits his pawn to open lines for your pieces. After a trade on d4, your rook gets the c column. [%cal Gc5d4] } (7... Be7 { Just as good: the bishop comes out first, and the pawn waits a move. } 8. Qe2 O-O 9. a3 { About level: you castled first, and your c pawn can still strike later. [%csl Gc7] }) (7... b6 { Just as good: the light bishop heads for the long diagonal first. [%cal Gc8b7,Gb7f3] } 8. Ne5 Bb7 9. Qe2 { About level: your bishop already stands on the long diagonal, and the c pawn can go later. [%cal Gb7g2] }) (7... Bd6 { The bishop comes out, but it has to move again once your c pawn goes. } 8. Qe2 c5 9. dxc5 Bxc5 { Slightly better for him: your bishop took two moves to reach c5, and he used the time. [%csl Rc5] }) (7... Bd7 { Just as good: the light bishop takes a small step first. } 8. Ne5 c5 9. dxc5 Bxc5 { About level: the c pawn still struck, one move later. [%csl Gc5] }) 8. dxc5 { He takes. Had he castled, or played a bishop move or his pawn to c3, you would take on d4 yourself. Take back with your bishop. [%cal Yf8c5] } 8... Bxc5 { Your bishop takes back and comes out at the same time. Against a check on b5, block it with your bishop; against his queen to e2, simply castle. [%cal Gc5f2] } (8... Qa5+ { The queen check wins the pawn back, but his bishop blocks with gain of time. } 9. Bd2 Qxc5 10. Qe2 { Slightly better for him: your queen moved twice to win back the pawn, and his pieces are ready to castle. [%csl Rc5] }) (8... Qc7 { The queen hits the pawn, but his b pawn steps up to guard it. } 9. b4 b6 10. Bb2 { About level, but he keeps the extra pawn for now. [%csl Rc5] }) (8... Be7 { The bishop comes out to e7 and leaves him the pawn. } 9. Qe2 Qc7 10. Bd2 { Slightly better for him: he is still a pawn up, and your bishop does less on e7 than it would on c5. [%csl Re7] }) 9. O-O { He castles. Castle too; had his bishop pinned your knight from g5 instead, castling was still the move. [%cal Ye8g8] } 9... O-O { Your king is safe, and every knight and bishop is out except your light bishop, still shut in behind the pawn on e6. [%csl Rc8] } (9... b6 { Just as good: the road for your bishop opens first, and you castle next. } 10. Bf4 Bb7 11. Qe2 { Level: your bishop stands on the long diagonal, and your king can castle next move. [%cal Gb7g2] }) (9... Qc7 { Just as good: the queen comes out first. } 10. Bg5 h6 11. Bh4 { Level: your king still has to castle, but his bishop was pushed back. [%csl Gh6] }) (9... Bd7 { Just as good, if a little quiet: the light bishop takes a small step. } 10. a3 a5 11. Ne5 { About level: his knight jumps in and hits your bishop, and your king has not castled yet. [%cal Re5d7] }) 10. Bg5 { His bishop pins your knight to your queen. Push your b pawn one step, so your light bishop can come to b7. Against his queen to e2, you would do the same. [%cal Yb7b6] } 10... b6 { The b pawn steps aside, and the long diagonal opens for your light bishop. Until the bishop gets there, your rook in the corner stands on that diagonal too. [%cal Gc8b7] } (10... h6 { Just as good: ask his bishop what it wants first. } 11. Bh4 b6 12. Qe2 { Level: his bishop stepped back, and your b pawn still made room for the bishop. [%csl Gb6] }) (10... Qc7 { Just as good: the queen steps out of the pin. } 11. Qd2 Be7 12. Bf4 { Level: the pin is gone, though his bishop now chases your queen. [%cal Rf4c7] }) (10... Be7 { Just as good: the bishop steps back to break the pin. } 11. Ne5 Qc7 12. Qe2 { Level: no pin any more, and your queen watches his knight on e5. [%cal Gc7e5] }) (10... Bd6 { The bishop steps back but leaves the pin in place. } 11. Bxf6 gxf6 12. Qd2 { About level, but his bishop took your knight and split your king side pawns. [%csl Rf6] }) 11. Qe2 { His queen comes out. Now your light bishop goes to b7, on the road the b pawn opened. Against his knight to e5, the bishop goes there all the same. [%cal Yc8b7] } 11... Bb7 { Your c pawn traded itself for his d pawn: that pawn break opened the game, and now your light bishop, shut in at the start, looks down the long diagonal. Level. [%cal Gb7f3] } (11... Qc7 { Just as good: the queen steps out of the pin first. } 12. Rad1 Bb7 13. Bxf6 gxf6 { Level: your king side pawns are split, but your two bishops make up for it. [%csl Rf6] }) (11... Qe7 { The queen steps up to e7, still behind your knight, and his bishop takes it anyway. } 12. Bxf6 gxf6 13. Rae1 { About level, but his bishop took the knight that guarded your king, and your king side pawns are split. [%csl Rf6] }) (11... Bd6 { The bishop steps back, but it lets his bishop take your knight. } 12. Bxf6 gxf6 13. Qe4 { Better for him: his queen hits your rook in the corner, and your king side pawns are broken. [%cal Re4a8] }) 0-1


[Event "French Defence: punish early castling"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/uIXK7n1G/tfozek4x"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[FEN "r1b1kb1r/pp3ppp/1qn1p2n/3pP3/3P4/5N2/PP2BPPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq - 1 8"]
[SetUp "1"]
