{ The French traps start here. You know this position from the main line: your knight has just jumped to the edge of the board. }
8. O-O { He castles, and that is a mistake: his knight to c3 was the move. Can you make him pay? } 8... Nf5 { The knight jumps to f5, and now three of your pieces hit d4. He guards it only twice. [%cal Gf5d4,Gc6d4,Gb6d4] } (8... Bd7 { The bishop comes out, but his dark bishop can still take your knight on h6. [%cal Rc1h6] } 9. Bxh6 gxh6 10. Nc3 { Better for him: his bishop took your knight, your king side pawns are split, and d4 is safe. [%csl Rh6] }) (8... Be7 { The bishop steps to e7, but your knight still waits on the edge. } 9. Bxh6 gxh6 10. Nc3 { Better for him: his bishop traded itself for your knight on h6, and your king side pawns are split. [%csl Rh6] }) (8... Bb4 { The bishop comes out to b4, but it hits nothing now that his king has castled. } 9. Bxh6 gxh6 10. Nc3 { Better for him: his bishop took your knight, and your bishop on b4 only wasted time. [%csl Rh6] }) (8... Nxd4 { Grabbing the pawn with the other knight runs into his knight and queen. } 9. Nxd4 Bd7 10. Nc3 { Much better for him: his knight took yours, and your queen cannot take back while his queen guards d4. [%cal Rd1d4] }) (8... g6 { Guarding f5 with a pawn first is slow. [%cal Gg6f5] } 9. Nc3 Nf5 10. Na4 Qd8 { Better for him: your knight reached f5, but then his knight chased your queen home, and d4 is safe. [%csl Rd8] }) (8... Bc5 { Look again: his d pawn takes the bishop. [%cal Rd4c5] }) (8... Bd6 { Look again: his pawn on e5 takes it. [%cal Re5d6] }) 9. Nc3 { His knight comes out, a natural move, and a queen move to a4 would not have saved him either. What now? } 9... Nfxd4 { The knight from f5 takes, and your knight on c6 still backs it up. Three attackers against two guards: the pawn could not be saved. [%cal Gc6d4] } (9... Ncxd4 { The other knight takes, but now his bishop can hit your knight on f5. } 10. Bd3 Nxf3+ 11. Qxf3 { Level: you are a pawn up, but his bishop and queen both hit your knight on f5, and his pieces are faster. [%csl Rf5] }) (9... Qxd4 { The queen grabs the pawn, but his knight on f3 guards it. } 10. Nxd4 Nfxd4 { Lost for you: your queen went for a knight and a pawn. [%csl Rd4] }) (9... Bd7 { Developing, but it gives his knight time to hit your queen. } 10. Na4 Qd8 { About level: his knight chased your queen home, and d4 is safe again. [%csl Gd4] }) (9... Be7 { The bishop steps out, and his knight jumps at your queen. } 10. Na4 Qd8 { About level: your queen had to run home, and the pawn on d4 got away. [%csl Gd4] }) (9... Bb4 { The bishop hits his knight on c3, but that knight does not guard d4 anyway. [%cal Gb4c3][%csl Gd4] } 10. Kh1 Bd7 11. g4 { Slightly better for him: his pawn chases your knight from f5, and d4 is safe. [%cal Rg4f5] }) (9... Bc5 { A bishop on c5 is simply taken by his d pawn. [%cal Rd4c5] }) (9... Bd6 { A bishop on d6 is simply taken by his e pawn. [%cal Re5d6] }) 10. Nxd4 { He takes back with his knight; hitting your queen from a4 would have lost even more. You have two ways to take back. Only one keeps your edge. } 10... Qxd4 { Your queen takes back, a pawn up. If he moves his queen or knight instead of trading, grab his e pawn; against his bishop to d3, keep his knight off b5 with your a pawn. [%cal Gd4e5] } (10... Nxd4 { The knight takes back, but his bishop comes to e3 and pins it to your queen. [%cal Rc1e3,Re3b6] } 11. Be3 Nxe2+ 12. Nxe2 { Level: still a pawn up, but his bishop now hits your queen, and his pieces come out fast. [%cal Re3b6] }) 11. Qxd4 { He trades queens; his knight to b5, hitting your queen and c7, was the tougher try. } 11... Nxd4 { Your knight takes back, and the queens are gone: you are a pawn up. Against a bishop move to d3, step back to c6; against the check, block with your bishop. } 12. Be3 { His bishop hits your knight, his best try. Where does the knight go? } 12... Nxe2+ { The knight takes his bishop with check, so it never has to run. Now watch. [%tail] } (12... Nc6 { Stepping back is safe, but his knight jumps to b5 with play. } 13. Nb5 Kd7 14. Rac1 { About level: still a pawn up, but his knight on b5 and his rook make trouble for your king. [%csl Rb5] }) (12... Nf5 { Going back to f5 lets his knight jump to b5 with play, and your knight ends up trading itself for his bishop. } 13. Nb5 Nxe3 14. fxe3 { About level: a pawn up, but his knight on b5 now hits c7. [%cal Rb5c7] }) (12... Bc5 { Guarding the knight with the bishop lets him start a chain of trades with a check. } 13. Bb5+ Nxb5 14. Nxb5 Bxe3 15. fxe3 { Level: two pairs of pieces came off, and his rook on the f column makes up for your extra pawn. [%csl Rf1] }) 13. Nxe2 b6 { A pawn up with the queens off, and your d pawn has no pawn left to stop it. Three attackers beat two guards on d4: that was the trap. [%csl Gd5] } 0-1


