{ Back in the gambit: you took his d pawn, and your pawn on a6 keeps his knight off b5. }
11. Be3 { His bishop hits your queen, and that is a mistake: his queen to e2 or his rook to e1 was the move. Where does your queen go? } 11... Qxe5 { Your queen escapes and grabs his e pawn on the way: two pawns up now. [%csl Ge5] } (11... Qb4 { The queen slides over to b4, safe, but empty handed. } 12. a3 Qa5 13. Qb3 { About level: his pawn chased your queen to the edge, and his pieces rule the board for the pawn. [%csl Ra5] }) (11... Qh4 { The queen runs to h4, far from the middle. } 12. f4 g6 13. Rc1 { About level: your queen is out of play on h4, and his rook takes the open c column. [%csl Rc1] }) (11... Qb6 { Look again: his bishop on e3 reaches b6 too. } 12. Bxb6 { Lost for you: his bishop took your queen. [%cal Re3b6] }) (11... Qc5 { Look again: his bishop on e3 reaches c5 too. } 12. Bxc5 { Lost for you: his bishop took your queen on c5. [%csl Rc5] }) (11... Qxe3 { Taking the bishop costs your queen: his f pawn takes back. } 12. fxe3 { Lost for you: your queen went for a bishop. [%csl Re3] }) 12. Re1 { His rook lines up behind his bishop, so when that bishop moves, your queen is hit. What now? } 12... Nf6 { Two pawns up, and your knight guards your king side. If his bishop moves and uncovers his rook, your queen just steps off the e column. [%csl Gf6] } (12... Be7 { Just as good: the bishop steps out, one move closer to castling. } 13. Rc1 Qd6 14. Na4 { Better for you: two pawns up, and your queen stepped off the e column in time. [%csl Gd6] }) (12... Qd6 { Also good: the queen steps off the e column at once. } 13. Bd4 Bc6 14. a4 { Better for you: two pawns up, though his bishop now sits on d4 in the middle. [%csl Rd4] }) (12... Bc6 { Just as good: the bishop comes out to c6. } 13. Ne2 Nf6 14. Nd4 { Better for you: two pawns up, but his knight found a fine square on d4. [%csl Rd4] }) (12... Rc8 { The rook takes the c column, but your king side pieces are still at home. } 13. Bc5 Qc7 14. Bd4 { Slightly better for you: two pawns up, but his bishop sits on the long diagonal, aiming at your king side. [%cal Rd4g7] }) (12... Bd6 { The bishop guards your queen, but a pawn push chases her. [%cal Gd6e5] } 13. f4 Qf6 14. Nxd5 { Slightly better for him: his pawn chased your queen, and his knight took back a pawn on d5. [%csl Rd5] }) (12... d4 { The pawn forks his knight and bishop, but his bishop steps back and uncovers his rook on your queen. } 13. Bd2 Qb8 14. Nd5 Nf6 { Level: your queen had to run, and his knight jumped into d5, but your knight on f6 now challenges it. [%csl Rd5] }) (12... Bb4 { The bishop comes out to b4, but his bishop moves to c5, hitting it, and uncovers his rook on your queen. [%cal Re3c5,Re1e5] } 13. Bc5 Bxc3 14. Rxe5 Bxe5 { Slightly better for him: your queen went for his rook and knight, and his pieces are the stronger. [%csl Re5] }) (12... O-O-O { Castling long walks your king toward his bishop. } 13. Ba7 Qd6 14. b4 Qxb4 { Much better for him: you are three pawns up, but his bishop on a7 and his rook, coming to the b column, go for your king. [%csl Ra7] }) (12... Ne7 { The knight comes out, but it shuts in your bishop. } 13. g3 Qd6 14. Rc1 { Slightly better for you: two pawns up, but your king side is slow to come out. [%csl Rf8] }) 0-1


