{ Another French trap. Your knight on b4 has just hit his bishop on c2. }
9. Bb3 { His bishop dodges to b3, and that is a mistake: castling was the move, and you could have the bishop. How do you punish it? } 9... Qa6 { Your queen now watches f1, so he cannot castle, and his king is stuck in the middle. [%cal Ga6f1] } (9... Bb5 { The bishop steps up to b5, the natural move, but his bishop simply offers a trade. } 10. Ba4 { About level: he offers to trade bishops on a4, and his king can still castle. [%cal Ra4b5] }) (9... Rc8 { The rook takes the c column, but that gives him time to cover his king. } 10. Nc3 Qa6 11. Qe2 { Level: your queen reached a6 a move too late, and his queen on e2 stands in her way. [%csl Re2] }) (9... Qb5 { The queen heads the right way, but by a slower road. } 10. Nc3 Qa6 11. Qe2 { Level: two queen moves to get there gave him time for his knight and his queen. [%csl Rc3] }) (9... Ne7 { Developing, but it lets him castle at once. } 10. O-O { Level: his king is safe, and the chance is gone. [%csl Rg1] }) (9... Nh6 { The knight heads for f5, as in the first game, but his dark bishop is still at home and takes it. [%cal Rc1h6] } 10. Bxh6 { About level: his bishop took your knight on h6 before it could reach f5. [%csl Rh6] }) (9... Nd3+ { The knight checks at once, but his queen simply takes it. } 10. Qxd3 { Lost for you: his queen took your knight for nothing. [%csl Rd3] }) (9... a5 { The a pawn props up your knight, but it is slow. } 10. a3 Nc6 11. Nc3 { Better for him: his pawn chased your knight back to c6, and he can castle in peace. [%csl Rc6] }) (9... Be7 { Developing the bishop is natural, but it lets him castle. } 10. O-O { About level: he castles, and the moment to catch his king in the middle has passed. [%csl Rg1] }) (9... O-O-O { Castling long puts your king on the other wing, and his king escapes too. } 10. O-O { About level: both kings are castled, and his is safe on the king side. [%csl Rg1] }) (9... Nf6 { The knight comes out, but his pawn on e5 hits that square. } 10. exf6 { Much better for him: his e pawn took your knight. [%csl Rf6] }) (9... Bc5 { The bishop hits d4, but it lands where his d pawn can take it. } 10. dxc5 { Lost for you: his d pawn took your bishop. [%csl Rc5] }) 10. Nc3 { He brings out his knight, a second mistake: his bishop had to go back to c2. His pawn to a3 would have lost the same way. What now? } 10... Nd3+ { Check! If his queen takes the knight, yours takes her, so his king has to move. [%cal Ga6d3] } (10... Ne7 { Developing, but it gives his queen time to step in front of his king. } 11. Qe2 Nd3+ 12. Kd2 { Level: his queen on e2 blocks your queen, and the knight check wins nothing now. [%csl Re2] }) (10... Rc8 { The rook comes to the c column, but he gets a move to defend. } 11. Qe2 Nd3+ 12. Kd2 { Level: his queen covers his king, and your knight check came a move too late. [%csl Rd2] }) (10... Nc2+ { A knight check that also hits his rook, but his bishop guards that square. } 11. Bxc2 { Lost for you: his bishop took your knight, and the fork was never real. [%csl Rc2] }) (10... Be7 { Developing, but it gives his queen time to step in. } 11. Qe2 Nd3+ 12. Kd1 { Level: his queen stepped to e2 in time, and his king found a square on d1. [%csl Gd1] }) (10... O-O-O { Castling first gives his queen time to offer a trade. } 11. Qe2 Qxe2+ 12. Kxe2 { Level: the queens came off, and his king is safe on e2. [%csl Re2] }) (10... Nf6 { The knight comes out, but his e pawn takes it. } 11. exf6 Nd3+ 12. Kd2 { Better for him: his pawn took your knight, and a piece down, your check does not win it back. [%csl Rf6] }) (10... Bc5 { The bishop hits d4, but his d pawn takes it. } 11. dxc5 Nd3+ 12. Kd2 { Better for him: his pawn took your bishop, and the check comes a piece too late. [%csl Rc5] }) 11. Kd2 { His king steps up to d2; on f1 it would walk into a discovered check from your queen. How do you cash in? } 11... Nxf2 { Your knight takes on f2 and forks his queen and his rook in the corner. [%cal Gf2d1,Gf2h1] } (11... Nxc1 { Taking the bishop is natural, but it lets his king off the hook. } 12. Rxc1 { About level: you traded your knight for his bishop, and his king got some air. [%csl Rc1] }) (11... Nxb2 { Grabbing a pawn with the knight walks into his bishop. } 12. Bxb2 { Much better for him: his bishop took your knight, and one pawn does not pay for it. [%csl Rb2] }) (11... Bb4 { The bishop pins his knight, but his king simply steps up. } 12. Ke3 Nxc1 13. Rxc1 { About level: his king chased your knight, and you had to trade it for his bishop. [%csl Rc1] }) (11... Nf4 { The knight steps back, and the whole attack fizzles out. } 12. Kc2 { About level: his king walked to safety, and you won nothing. [%csl Rc2] }) (11... Ne7 { Developing, but it lets his queen come to e2. } 12. Qe2 Nxc1 13. Rhxc1 { Level: his queen came to e2, and your knight had to settle for his bishop. [%csl Rc1] }) (11... Be7 { A quiet bishop move gives his queen time. } 12. Qe2 Nxc1 13. Raxc1 { Level: you traded your knight for his bishop, and his queen now guards his king. [%csl Re2] }) (11... O-O-O { Castling makes your king safe, but his king gets a breath too. } 12. Qe2 Nxc1 13. Rhxc1 { Level: your king is safe, but so is his, now that his queen stands on e2. [%csl Re2] }) (11... Nf6 { The knight develops, but it steps onto a square his e pawn hits. } 12. exf6 { Better for him: his pawn took your knight on f6, and a piece down, your attack runs out of steam. [%csl Rf6] }) 12. Qf1 { He saves his queen on f1, where she also hits yours; on e2 she would do no better. What comes first? } 12... Nxh1 { Your knight grabs the rook in the corner first, and your queen is still guarded by your b pawn. Now watch. [%tail] } (12... Qxf1 { Trading queens first lets his rook take back. } 13. Rxf1 Ne4+ 14. Nxe4 dxe4 { Slightly better for you: only a pawn up, because his rook took back on f1. [%csl Rf1] }) (12... Ne4+ { A check, but his knight simply takes yours. } 13. Nxe4 Bb5 14. Qe1 dxe4 15. Qxe4 { Better for you: the material is level, but your bishop chased his queen first, so the queens stay on with his king stuck in the middle. [%csl Rd2] }) (12... Ng4 { The knight steps back and lets him trade queens. } 13. Qxa6 bxa6 14. h3 { Level: the queens came off, and your chance to win material went with them. [%csl Rg4] }) (12... Bb4 { The bishop pins his knight, but your own knight is hanging. } 13. Qxf2 { Much better for him: his queen took your knight on f2. [%csl Rf2] }) (12... Ne7 { Developing, but it leaves your knight where his queen can take it. } 13. Qxf2 { Much better for him: you forgot your knight, and his queen took it. [%csl Rf2] }) (12... O-O-O { Castling leaves your knight to his queen. } 13. Qxf2 { Much better for him: castling left your knight on f2 to his queen. [%csl Rf2] }) 13. Qxh1 Bb4 { A rook and a pawn for your knight, and your bishop now pins his knight on c3. His bishop's trip to b3 let your queen shut his king in the middle. [%cal Gb4c3] } 0-1


