) (10... Qxb1+ { The queen grabs the knight with check, but his queen takes her. } 11. Qxb1 { Lost for you: your queen went after his knight, and his queen took her. [%csl Rb1] }) (10... Bd7 { Developing, but his bishop simply shuts the door. } 11. Bc3 Bb4 12. O-O { Much better for him: his bishop on c3 cuts your queen off, and she cannot get home. [%csl Rc3] }) (10... Be7 { Developing, but his bishop shuts the door on your queen. } 11. Bc3 Qxc3+ 12. Nxc3 { Lost for you: your queen had to give herself up for his bishop. [%csl Rc3] }) (10... Bc5 { The bishop hits d4, but your queen is still stuck in the corner. } 11. Bc3 Bb4 12. O-O { Lost for you: his bishop on c3 traps your queen in the corner. [%csl Rc3] }) 11. Nxd4 { His knight takes yours, and your queen is still in the corner. What now? } 11... Qxd4 { Your queen is back in the middle, and you have a rook and two pawns for a knight. [%cal Ga1d4] } (11... Bc5 { The bishop joins in, but your queen stays stuck while his knight lives. } 12. Bc3 Bxd4 13. Bxa1 { Lost for you: his bishop took your queen in the corner. [%csl Ra1] }) (11... Bd7 { Developing instead of taking back leaves your queen stuck. } 12. Bc3 { Lost for you: you did not take back, and his bishop traps your queen. [%csl Rc3] }) (11... Be7 { The bishop comes out, but your queen stays in the corner. } 12. Bb5+ Kf8 13. Bc3 { Lost for you: his light bishop checks, then his dark bishop shuts your queen in the corner for good. [%csl Ra1] }) 12. Bb5+ { His bishop gives check from b5. How do you meet it? } 12... Kd8 { Your king steps aside to d8, and your queen keeps the extra material. A bishop block would have let him trade it off and chase your king. [%csl Gd8] } (12... Bd7 { Blocking with the bishop looks safe, but he takes it with check. } 13. Bxd7+ Kd8 14. O-O { Much better for him: your bishop is gone, your king is stuck in the middle, and his pieces are all around it. [%csl Rd8] }) (12... Ke7 { The king steps up to e7, where it blocks your own bishop. } 13. O-O { Slightly better for you, but your king on e7 blocks your bishop, and his rook joins the attack. [%csl Re7] }) 13. O-O { He castles, and his rook joins the attack on your king. Keep your extra material safe: what now? } 13... Qxe5 { Your queen takes the loose pawn on e5 and comes back to guard c7, next to your king. [%cal Ge5c7] } (13... Bc5 { Also strong: the bishop comes out and guards your queen, but his pawn on e5 lives. } 14. Qf3 Ke7 15. Qg3 { Better for you: you keep a rook and two pawns for a knight, and your king has found e7. [%csl Ge7] }) (13... a6 { Chasing his bishop gives him time to go after your king. } 14. Be3 Qxe5 15. Bb6+ { Level: his bishops chase your king, and your extra material no longer counts. [%csl Rb6] }) (13... Qb6 { The queen runs back, but his queen and bishop can team up to pin her to your king from a5. [%cal Rd2a5] } 14. Qa4 a5 15. Rc1 { Better for him: your queen went back, and his rook joins the hunt for your king. [%csl Rc1] }) (13... Be7 { A quiet move that lets his bishop chase your queen. } 14. Bc3 Qb6 15. Qa4 { Better for you: you are still well ahead, but his pieces keep chasing your queen and king. [%csl Ra4] }) (13... Bd7 { Your bishop comes out, but he takes it, and your king cannot take back: his queen would then check on b7. } 14. Bxd7 Qb6 15. Qa4 { Much better for him: your bishop is gone, and his pieces close in on your king in the middle. [%csl Rd8] }) (13... Bb4 { The bishop hits his bishop, but his bishop takes first. } 14. Bxb4 Qxe5 15. Bc3 { Lost for you: he took your bishop, and his pieces swarm around your king. [%csl Rc3] }) 14. Ba5+ { His bishop checks from a5; a quiet rook move was stronger. How do you answer? } 14... b6 { Your b pawn blocks the check and hits his bishop at the same time. Now watch. [%cal Gb6a5][%tail] } (14... Ke7 { The king steps up, but his rook comes to the e column and hits your queen. [%cal Rf1e1,Re1e5] } 15. Re1 Qf4 16. Nc3 { Lost for you: his rook and knight join the attack, and your king on e7 is caught in the open. [%csl Re7] }) (14... Qc7 { Blocking with the queen puts her where his bishop can take her. } 15. Bxc7+ Kxc7 { Lost for you: you gave your queen to stop one check. [%csl Rc7] }) 15. Bc3 Qd6 { A rook and three pawns for a knight: his bishop ran back to d2 and left the rook in the corner, and your queen found the way home through d4. [%csl Gd6] } 0-1


