10. d5 { His isolated pawn runs forward and hits your knight. It looks active, and it is a mistake: his bishop to e3 was right. What now? } 10... Ne5 { Your knight steps forward and hits his knight and his bishop at once. [%cal Ge5f3,Ge5c4] } (10... Bxf3 { Just as good: take his knight first, then your knight jumps to e5 and hits his queen. } 11. Qxf3 Ne5 { Slightly better for you: your knight hits his queen and his bishop, and he must keep guarding d5. [%cal Ge5f3,Ge5c4][%csl Rd5] }) (10... Na5 { Just as good: the knight hits his bishop from the edge. } 11. Be2 Bxc3 12. bxc3 Qxd5 { Slightly better for you: you won the pawn by another road. [%csl Gd5] }) (10... Ne7 { The knight steps back and lets his pawn live. } 11. h3 Bh5 { Level: his pawn on d5 stands firm, and the chance to win it is gone. [%csl Rd5] }) (10... Nxd5 { Grabbing the pawn with the knight walks into his bishop. } 11. Bxd5 { Lost for you: his bishop took your knight, and a pawn does not pay for a knight. [%csl Rd5] }) (10... Bxc3 { Taking his knight lets his pawn take yours. } 11. dxc6 Qxd1 12. Rxd1 { About level: the trades cancel out, and his pawn on c6 digs into your position. [%csl Rc6] }) (10... Qxd5 { The queen grabs the pawn, right in front of his knight. } 11. Nxd5 { Lost for you: his knight took your queen. [%csl Rd5] }) (10... Nd4 { The knight jumps into the middle, where his queen can take it. } 11. Qxd4 Bxf3 12. gxf3 { Lost for you: his queen took your knight, and after the trade on f3 you are a piece down. [%csl Rd4] }) 11. Be2 { His bishop steps back and guards his knight, and now that knight is free to take yours on e5. What now? } 11... Bxf3 { You take his knight on f3, and the trades begin. [%csl Gf3] } (11... Bxc3 { Just as good: take the other knight first. } 12. bxc3 { Slightly better for you: the knight that guarded his pawn is gone. [%csl Gd5] }) (11... Nxf3+ { Just as good: the knight takes first, with check. } 12. Bxf3 { Slightly better for you: the same trades in another order. [%csl Gf3] }) (11... Ng6 { The knight steps back and lets him off. } 12. h3 Bxf3 13. Bxf3 { Level: his pawn on d5 keeps its guards, and the chance passed. [%csl Rd5] }) (11... Re8 { The rook takes the open column and guards your knight, but his knight takes it anyway: if your rook takes back, his bishop takes yours on g4. } 12. Nxe5 Bxe2 13. Qxe2 Bxc3 14. bxc3 { Slightly better for him: you are a knight down for now, but your rook pins his knight to his queen, so you can win it back. [%cal Re8e2] }) (11... c6 { The c pawn hits his pawn, but your knight on e5 is left hanging. } 12. Nxe5 Bxe2 13. Nxe2 { Lost for you: while your pawn pushed, his knight took yours, and you are a piece down. [%csl Re5] }) (11... Qe7 { The queen guards your knight, but his knight takes it anyway, since taking back would drop your bishop on g4. } 12. Nxe5 Bxe2 13. Qxe2 { Much better for him: his knight took yours, and his queen guards it from e2. [%cal Re2e5] }) (11... Qxd5 { The queen grabs the pawn, but a knight still guards it. } 12. Nxd5 { Lost for you: the pawn was guarded by his knight, which took your queen. [%csl Rd5] }) (11... c5 { The c pawn pushes past, and your knight on e5 still hangs. } 12. Nxe5 Bxe2 13. Nxe2 { Lost for you: his knight took yours on e5, and the trades after it leave you a piece down. [%csl Re5] }) 12. Bxf3 { His bishop takes back on f3. What now? } 12... Nxf3+ { Your knight takes the bishop, with check. [%csl Gf3] } (12... Bxc3 { Just as good: take the knight on c3 first. } 13. bxc3 Nxf3+ 14. Qxf3 Qxd5 { Slightly better for you: a pawn up, won in another order. [%csl Gd5] }) (12... h6 { Also fine: a quiet move first, and the trades can wait. } 13. Rb1 b6 14. Ne4 { About level: his knight left c3 before you could take it, and his pawn lives on. [%csl Rd5] }) (12... Re8 { Also good: your rook takes the open column first. } 13. Rb1 b6 14. d6 { Slightly better for you, but his pawn ran on to d6 instead of standing still to be taken. [%csl Gd6] }) (12... Qd7 { Also good: the queen lines up on his pawn. } 13. Be2 Bxc3 14. bxc3 Qxd5 { About level: you won the pawn, but his bishop got away from your knight. [%csl Gd5] }) (12... c6 { The c pawn hits his pawn, and he simply takes it. } 13. dxc6 Qxd1 14. Bxd1 { Level: the queens came off, and his pawn on c6 took the place of the one you wanted. [%csl Rc6] }) (12... Qxd5 { The queen takes the pawn, forgetting his knight on c3. } 13. Nxd5 { Lost for you: his knight took your queen on d5, and your knight trade on f3 comes too late. [%csl Rd5] }) (12... Qe7 { A quiet queen move gives him a moment, and your edge is gone. } 13. g3 { Level: he got a free move, and his pawn on d5 still stands. [%csl Rd5] }) 13. Qxf3 { His queen takes back, and his pawn on d5 still stands. What now? } 13... Bxc3 { Your bishop takes the knight that guarded d5. If his queen takes back, your knight takes the pawn on d5. } (13... Re8 { Your rook takes the open column, but the knight on c3 still guards his pawn. } 14. Bg5 Bxc3 15. Qxc3 { About level: his queen took back, and his pawn on d5 is safe. [%csl Rd5] }) (13... Qd7 { The queen lines up on the pawn, but it has two guards. } 14. Rd1 { Level: his rook came to guard it a third time, and the chance is gone. [%csl Rd5] }) (13... h6 { A quiet move, and he uses the time to guard his pawn. } 14. Rd1 { Level: his rook joined the guards of the pawn on d5. [%cal Rd1d5] }) (13... Bd6 { The bishop steps back, and his knight on c3 keeps guarding the pawn. } 14. Bg5 { Level: his bishop pins your knight, and his pawn is safe. [%cal Rg5f6] }) (13... c6 { The c pawn hits his pawn, and the pawns just trade. } 14. dxc6 bxc6 { About level: the pawns traded, and nothing is left to win on d5. [%csl Rc6] }) (13... Qe7 { The queen comes out, and his bishop pins your knight to her. } 14. Bg5 { Level: the pin holds your knight, and his pawn on d5 is safe. [%cal Rg5e7] }) (13... Qxd5 { The queen grabs the pawn while his knight still guards it. } 14. Nxd5 { Lost for you: the knight on c3 was still there, and it took your queen. [%csl Rd5] }) 14. bxc3 { His pawn takes back, and his pawns are split. What now? } 14... Qxd5 { Your queen takes the pawn: its knight guard is gone, and if his queen takes yours, your knight takes back. Now watch. [%tail] } (14... Re8 { Your rook takes the open column, but he has time to guard his pawn. } 15. c4 { Level: his c pawn came up to guard d5, and the chance is gone. [%csl Rd5] }) (14... Nxd5 { Taking with the knight leaves your rook on f8 to his bishop. } 15. Ba3 c6 16. Bxf8 Qxf8 { Much better for him: you took a pawn, but his bishop won your rook for it. [%csl Rf8] }) (14... Qd6 { The queen hits the pawn once more, but he guards it first. } 15. c4 { Level: his c pawn guards the pawn on d5 now, and your chance to win it has passed. [%csl Rd5] }) (14... c6 { The c pawn hits his pawn, but the trade opens your c pawn to his queen. } 15. dxc6 bxc6 16. Qxc6 { About level, but his queen won a pawn on c6. [%csl Rc6] }) (14... c5 { The c pawn steps up to c5, and now none of your pawns can ever stop his pawn on d5. } 15. c4 b5 16. Bf4 { Slightly better for him: his pawn on d5 is passed now, with a pawn behind it for support. [%csl Rd5] }) (14... Qd7 { The queen lines up on the pawn, a move too slow. } 15. c4 Rfe8 16. Bb2 { About level: his c pawn guards d5, and his bishop joined in on the long diagonal. [%cal Rc4d5,Rb2f6] }) (14... Qe7 { The queen steps to e7, and he guards his pawn with the c pawn. } 15. c4 { Level: the pawn on d5 has a pawn guard now, and the chance is gone. [%csl Rd5] }) 15. Qxd5 Nxd5 { A pawn up, with the queens off. You traded off the bishop and the knight that guarded his d pawn, and then you took it. [%csl Gd5] } 0-1


