{ A trap from the pawn break chapter. Your b pawn has just stepped up to make room for your light bishop. }
11. Be4 { His bishop hits your rook in the corner. It looks strong, and it is a mistake: his rook to e1 was the calm move. What now? } 11... Qxd1 { You take his queen first: his bishop left the d column, and your queens faced each other. If his bishop grabs your rook now, keep your queen: take his c pawn with her. [%cal Gd8d1] } (11... Rb8 { Saving the rook is natural, but a whole piece was there for the taking. } 12. Qe2 { Level: your rook is safe, and the chance is gone. [%csl Rb8] }) (11... Nxe4 { Taking the bishop at once opens the road from his bishop on g5 to your queen. } 12. Bxd8 { Lost for you: his bishop took your queen, because your knight no longer stood in its way. [%csl Rd8] }) (11... Ba6 { Hitting his rook back leads to a long run of trades. } 12. Bxa8 Bxf1 13. Qxd8 Rxd8 14. Kxf1 Rxa8 { Level: every trade cancels out, and the extra piece never came. [%csl Ra8] }) (11... Bb7 { Blocking with the bishop just loses it. } 12. Bxb7 Rb8 13. Qxd8 Rfxd8 { Lost for you: his bishop took yours, and after the queens came off you are a piece down. [%csl Rb7] }) (11... Bxf2+ { A check first also wins, but less. } 12. Rxf2 Qxd1+ 13. Rxd1 Nxe4 { Better for you: a pawn up, and your knight hits his rook and bishop together. Taking the queens first wins even more. [%cal Ge4f2,Ge4g5] }) (11... Qc7 { The queen steps out of the pin, but your rook is still attacked. } 12. Bxa8 { Lost for you: his bishop took your rook in the corner. [%csl Ra8] }) (11... Qe7 { The queen steps up to e7, but his bishop still takes your rook in the corner. } 12. Bxa8 { Lost for you: his bishop ate your rook, and the queens are still on. [%csl Ra8] }) (11... Bd7 { The bishop comes out, but your rook in the corner is still attacked. } 12. Bxa8 Qc7 13. Bxf6 gxf6 { Lost for you: his bishop took your rook in the corner, and the trade on f6 changed nothing. [%csl Ra8] }) (11... e5 { The e pawn pushes, and it does nothing for your rook in the corner. } 12. Bxa8 Qc7 13. Bxf6 gxf6 { Lost for you: the pawn push left your rook to his bishop, and you are a whole rook down. [%csl Ra8] }) 12. Raxd1 { He takes back with a rook; taking on h7 with check first would have lost less. Your rook in the corner is still attacked. What now? } 12... Nxe4 { Your knight takes the bishop that hit your rook, and you are a whole piece up. The queens came off first, so his bishop on g5 had nothing left to take. [%csl Ge4] } (12... Rb8 { Saving the rook lets his bishop off the hook. } 13. Bf4 { Level: his other bishop hits your rook, and taking on e4 now would cost you that rook. [%cal Rf4b8] }) (12... Bxf2+ { A check first, but it gives back too much. } 13. Kxf2 Nxe4+ 14. Ke3 { Better for you: a pawn up, but taking the bishop at once kept a whole piece. [%csl Ge4] }) (12... Ba6 { Hitting his rook back only trades everything off. } 13. Bxf6 gxf6 14. Bxa8 Bxf1 15. Kxf1 Rxa8 { About level: a rook and two minor pieces came off each side, and the whole piece you could have won is gone. [%csl Ra8] }) (12... Bb7 { Blocking with the bishop gives it away. } 13. Bxb7 Rab8 14. Bxf6 gxf6 { Lost for you: his bishop took yours, his other bishop traded itself for your knight, and you are a piece down. [%csl Rb7] }) (12... Nd5 { The knight runs, and his bishop gets away. } 13. c4 f5 14. Bxd5 exd5 { Slightly better for him: his bishop swapped itself off for your knight, and your pawn on d5 is weak. [%csl Rd5] }) (12... Bd7 { The bishop comes out, but it stands on the open d column. } 13. Bxf6 gxf6 14. Bxa8 Rxa8 15. Rxd7 { Lost for you: his bishop took your rook, and then his rook took your bishop on d7. [%csl Rd7] }) (12... e5 { The e pawn pushes, and your rook in the corner is still attacked. } 13. Bxf6 gxf6 14. Bxa8 { Lost for you: his bishop took your rook in the corner, and nothing can take it back. [%csl Ra8] }) 0-1
